import { Notes } from '../types';

// AUTO-GENERATED study notes keyed by component id. Do not hand-edit.

export const STUDY_NOTES: Record<string, Notes> = {
  "econ-basic": {"summary": "Basic economic concepts on the Series 65 test how macro forces shape markets and client portfolios. The business cycle moves through expansion, peak, contraction (recession), and trough; defensive sectors (utilities, consumer staples) tend to hold up better in downturns while cyclicals lead recoveries. Analysts judge where the economy sits using indicators grouped as leading, coincident, and lagging.\n\nPolicymakers steer the economy with two distinct levers, and the exam loves to test which body controls which. Monetary policy is the Federal Reserve's control of money and credit through open market operations, the discount rate, and reserve requirements — the Fed buying securities or cutting rates is expansionary, selling securities or raising rates is contractionary. Fiscal policy is Congress and the President's use of government spending and taxation.\n\nPrices and rates connect the macro picture to investments. Inflation (measured by the CPI) erodes purchasing power and hurts lenders and fixed-income investors, while borrowers repaying fixed-rate debt benefit; deflation raises the real burden of debt and can suppress spending. Interest rates and the shape of the yield curve reflect growth and inflation expectations — an inverted curve (short yields above long) has historically warned of recession, and widening credit spreads signal rising default fears and a flight to quality.\n\nGlobal factors round out the topic. Exchange rates directly affect the dollar return on foreign assets: a stronger dollar reduces the dollar value of foreign holdings, a weaker dollar boosts it. The balance of payments nets the current account (which includes the trade balance) against the capital/financial account, so a trade deficit is offset by capital inflows. Sovereign-debt concerns can widen a country's bond spreads and weaken its currency at the same time.", "keyPoints": ["Business cycle phases in order: expansion -> peak -> contraction (recession) -> trough; a recession is commonly defined as two consecutive quarters of declining real GDP.", "Monetary policy is run by the Fed (open market operations, discount rate, reserve requirements); fiscal policy is set by Congress and the President (government spending and taxation).", "Fed BUYING securities is expansionary (adds reserves, expands the money supply, lowers short-term rates); SELLING securities is contractionary.", "Lowering the discount rate or reserve requirement is expansionary; raising either is contractionary.", "Leading indicators: stock prices, building permits, new manufacturing orders, initial jobless claims, M2 money supply, and the yield-curve slope. Coincident: GDP, industrial production, nonfarm payrolls, personal income. Lagging: the unemployment rate, CPI, the prime rate, and average duration of unemployment.", "The CPI measures consumer-price inflation; inflation erodes purchasing power, hurting lenders and holders of long-term fixed-coupon bonds, while borrowers with fixed-rate debt benefit.", "Real return is approximately the nominal yield minus the inflation rate, and it can be negative when inflation exceeds the nominal yield.", "Deflation is a sustained decline in the general price level; it increases the real value of debt and can depress consumer spending as buyers wait for lower prices.", "A normal yield curve slopes upward (long-term yields above short-term); an inverted curve (short above long) has historically preceded recessions; a flat curve signals a transition.", "A credit (yield) spread is the extra yield of a bond over a comparable-maturity Treasury; spreads WIDEN in recessions/risk-off periods (flight to quality) and NARROW during expansions.", "For a US investor in foreign assets, appreciation of the foreign currency (a weaker dollar) adds to the dollar return, while a stronger dollar reduces it.", "Balance of payments = current account (trade in goods and services plus income) + capital/financial account; a trade/current-account deficit is offset by a capital-account surplus, so the overall balance nets to roughly zero.", "A weaker domestic currency tends to make exports cheaper abroad (boosting them) and raises import prices; a stronger currency does the reverse.", "Sovereign-debt risk reflects a government's ability and willingness to repay; rising default risk widens sovereign spreads and can weaken the country's currency."], "formulas": ["Real interest rate (Fisher approximation) = Nominal rate - Inflation rate", "Approximate USD return on a foreign asset = (1 + local-currency return) x (1 + currency return) - 1", "GDP = C + I + G + (X - M), where X - M is net exports", "Recession rule of thumb: two consecutive quarters of declining real GDP", "Credit spread = Yield on a bond - Yield on a comparable-maturity Treasury"], "mnemonics": ["Business cycle phases 'Every Peak Comes Tumbling' = Expansion, Peak, Contraction, Trough.", "Fed action: 'Buy = Boost' (buying securities boosts the money supply, expansionary); 'Sell = Squeeze' (contractionary).", "Who does what: Fiscal = Congress (spending and taxes); Monetary = the Fed (money supply and rates).", "LEADING indicators let you LOOK AHEAD: stock prices, building permits, new orders, initial jobless claims, M2, yield curve."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing monetary policy (the Fed) with fiscal policy (Congress and the President).", "Assuming the Fed selling securities is expansionary - it is contractionary; buying is expansionary.", "Mixing up the unemployment RATE (a lagging indicator) with initial jobless CLAIMS (a leading indicator); CPI is also lagging.", "Believing a strong local-currency return guarantees a strong dollar return - currency depreciation can erase or reverse it.", "Forgetting that a real return can be negative when inflation exceeds the nominal yield.", "Thinking an increase in the reserve requirement is expansionary - it is contractionary because it shrinks the money multiplier.", "Treating a trade deficit as an unbalanced economy - a current-account deficit is offset by a capital/financial-account surplus.", "Reading an inverted yield curve as a sign of expansion - short yields above long yields is a classic recession warning.", "Interpreting widening credit spreads as economic strength - widening spreads reflect rising perceived default risk (risk-off)."]},
  "econ-reporting": {"summary": "Financial reporting gives an investment adviser the raw material to judge a company's health, so a Series 65 candidate must know what each of the three primary statements shows and how they connect. The income statement (profit and loss statement) covers a PERIOD of time and reports revenues minus expenses to arrive at net income; it is built on accrual accounting. The balance sheet is a SNAPSHOT at a single point in time and always obeys the identity Assets = Liabilities + Shareholders' Equity. The statement of cash flows reconciles net income to actual cash, breaking movement into operating, investing, and financing activities and explaining why a profitable company can still run short of cash.\n\nPublic companies file these statements with the SEC. The annual report on Form 10-K contains audited financials plus management's discussion and analysis (MD&A) and disclosures; the quarterly Form 10-Q is UNAUDITED (reviewed, not audited); and material events between periodic reports are disclosed on Form 8-K. Audited statements carry an independent CPA's opinion. The best outcome is an unqualified (clean) opinion; a qualified opinion flags a specific exception; an adverse opinion says the statements are NOT fairly presented; and a disclaimer means the auditor could not form an opinion. An audit provides reasonable, not absolute, assurance and does not guarantee the company is a good investment or free of fraud.\n\nAccrual accounting recognizes revenue when earned and expenses when incurred, regardless of when cash changes hands, and is required under GAAP for public companies. Cash-basis accounting records revenue and expense only when cash is actually received or paid; it is simpler and common for very small businesses but can distort the timing of performance. Understanding the difference explains items like accounts receivable, accounts payable, deferred revenue, and depreciation, all of which arise under accrual but not pure cash accounting.", "keyPoints": ["Balance sheet is a point-in-time snapshot; income statement and statement of cash flows both cover a period of time.", "The fundamental accounting equation: Assets = Liabilities + Shareholders' Equity (net worth).", "Income statement flows from revenue down through operating income (EBIT), pretax income, and finally net income (the bottom line).", "Statement of cash flows has three sections: operating, investing, and financing activities.", "A profitable company (positive net income) can still have negative cash flow, and vice versa, because of accrual timing and non-cash items.", "Form 10-K = annual, audited; Form 10-Q = quarterly, unaudited (reviewed); Form 8-K = current report of material events.", "Audit opinions ranked best to worst: unqualified (clean), qualified, adverse, disclaimer of opinion.", "An audit gives reasonable assurance that statements are fairly presented under GAAP; it is not a guarantee against fraud or a judgment on investment merit.", "Accrual accounting: recognize revenue when earned and expenses when incurred, regardless of cash movement; required by GAAP.", "Cash-basis accounting: recognize revenue and expenses only when cash is received or paid.", "Accounts receivable, accounts payable, deferred/unearned revenue, and depreciation exist under accrual accounting, not pure cash accounting.", "Depreciation is a non-cash expense: it lowers net income on the income statement but is added back to cash in the operating section of the cash flow statement.", "Shareholders' equity (book value / net worth) = total assets minus total liabilities.", "MD&A (management's discussion and analysis) is management's narrative and is part of the 10-K, distinct from the audited financial statements themselves."], "formulas": ["Assets = Liabilities + Shareholders' Equity", "Shareholders' Equity (Net Worth / Book Value) = Total Assets - Total Liabilities", "Net Income = Revenues - Expenses (including COGS, operating expenses, interest, and taxes)", "Working Capital = Current Assets - Current Liabilities", "Change in Cash = Cash Flow from Operations + Cash Flow from Investing + Cash Flow from Financing"], "mnemonics": ["Statement of cash flows sections = 'OIF': Operating, Investing, Financing.", "Audit opinion quality, best to worst: 'Ugly Quilts Are Dreadful' = Unqualified, Qualified, Adverse, Disclaimer.", "Balance sheet = a Photo (a moment); Income statement = a Movie (a period)."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing the 'unqualified' opinion with a bad result. Unqualified is actually the BEST (clean) opinion; a QUALIFIED opinion is the one that flags a problem.", "Assuming the 10-Q is audited. Quarterly statements are reviewed, NOT audited; only the annual 10-K carries a full audit opinion.", "Treating net income and cash flow as the same thing. Accrual timing and non-cash items like depreciation make them differ.", "Thinking an audit guarantees no fraud or endorses the stock. An audit gives only reasonable assurance of fair presentation under GAAP.", "Putting depreciation in the wrong place. It reduces net income (income statement) but is a non-cash charge added back in operating cash flow.", "Calling the balance sheet a period statement. It is a single-date snapshot, while the income statement and cash flow statement span a period."]},
  "econ-analytical": {"summary": "Analytical methods on the Series 65 fall into four buckets: time value of money, descriptive statistics, financial (liquidity/leverage) ratios, and valuation ratios. Time value of money underlies net present value (NPV) and internal rate of return (IRR). NPV discounts all future cash flows back to today at a required rate and subtracts the initial cost; a positive NPV means the investment earns more than the discount rate. IRR is the discount rate that makes NPV equal to zero, i.e., the annualized compound return implied by the cash flows. An investment is attractive when IRR exceeds the required (discount) rate, which is exactly when NPV is positive.\n\nDescriptive statistics summarize return distributions and risk. Mean, median, and mode describe central tendency; range and standard deviation describe dispersion. Standard deviation measures total (absolute) volatility, while beta measures systematic (market-related) volatility relative to a benchmark. Alpha is the return earned above or below what beta/CAPM would predict. The Sharpe ratio measures risk-adjusted return as excess return per unit of total risk (standard deviation). Correlation (ranging from -1 to +1) measures how two assets move together and drives diversification benefits.\n\nFinancial ratios assess a company's health. Liquidity ratios (current ratio, quick/acid-test ratio) gauge the ability to pay short-term obligations; leverage ratios (debt-to-equity) gauge reliance on borrowed capital. Valuation ratios relate market price to fundamentals: price-to-earnings (P/E) compares price to earnings per share, and price-to-book (P/B) compares price to net book value per share. High P/E often signals growth expectations; low P/B can signal a value or distressed situation. These are relative measures, most meaningful when compared to peers, the industry, or the company's own history.", "keyPoints": ["NPV = present value of all future cash flows discounted at the required rate, minus the initial investment; accept a project when NPV is positive.", "IRR is the discount rate that makes NPV = 0; it is the annualized compound (dollar-weighted) return implied by the cash flows.", "Decision rule linkage: NPV > 0 exactly when IRR > required rate of return (discount rate); the two rules agree for a single conventional investment.", "Standard deviation measures total risk (both systematic and unsystematic); beta measures only systematic (non-diversifiable) market risk.", "Beta of 1.0 moves with the market; beta > 1 is more volatile than the market; beta < 1 is less volatile; the market's beta is 1.0.", "Alpha is realized return minus CAPM-expected return; positive alpha means outperformance versus what beta predicted.", "Sharpe ratio = (portfolio return - risk-free rate) / standard deviation; higher is better risk-adjusted performance; it uses total risk, not beta.", "Mean is affected by outliers; median (middle value) is more representative of a skewed distribution; mode is the most frequently occurring value.", "Correlation ranges from -1 to +1; the lower (more negative) the correlation between assets, the greater the diversification benefit.", "Current ratio = current assets / current liabilities; quick (acid-test) ratio excludes inventory and is a stricter liquidity test.", "Debt-to-equity ratio = total debt / total (shareholders') equity; higher ratios indicate greater financial leverage and risk.", "P/E ratio = market price per share / earnings per share; a high P/E generally reflects higher growth expectations.", "P/B ratio = market price per share / book value per share; book value = (assets - liabilities) per share; P/B < 1 means price is below net book value.", "Valuation and financial ratios are relative tools, most useful compared against industry peers, the sector, or historical values."], "formulas": ["NPV = Σ [CFt / (1 + r)^t] - Initial Investment", "IRR: the rate r such that Σ [CFt / (1 + r)^t] - Initial Investment = 0 (NPV = 0)", "Sharpe Ratio = (Rp - Rf) / σp", "Alpha = Actual Return - [Rf + β(Rm - Rf)] (CAPM expected return)", "Current Ratio = Current Assets / Current Liabilities", "Quick Ratio = (Current Assets - Inventory) / Current Liabilities", "Debt-to-Equity = Total Debt / Total Shareholders' Equity", "P/E = Market Price per Share / Earnings per Share (EPS)", "P/B = Market Price per Share / Book Value per Share", "Book Value per Share = (Total Assets - Total Liabilities) / Shares Outstanding"], "mnemonics": ["'IRR is the rate that zeroes the NPV' - memorize the link, not two separate rules.", "'Sharpe uses Sigma' - Sharpe ratio denominator is standard deviation (total risk); Treynor uses beta.", "'Quick = Current minus Inventory' - inventory is the least liquid current asset, so the acid test drops it.", "Central tendency order: Mean (average, outlier-sensitive), Median (middle), Mode (most frequent)."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing standard deviation (total risk) with beta (systematic/market risk only) - Sharpe uses standard deviation; Treynor uses beta.", "Thinking a higher IRR always means a better project - IRR can mislead with unconventional cash flows or when comparing mutually exclusive projects of different scale; NPV is the more reliable decision tool.", "Assuming NPV and IRR give opposite signals - for one conventional investment, NPV > 0 always coincides with IRR > discount rate.", "Believing a high P/E means a stock is 'overpriced' or a low P/E means a 'bargain' - P/E reflects growth expectations and must be compared to peers.", "Forgetting the quick ratio excludes inventory; using total current assets defeats the purpose of the acid test.", "Assuming positive correlation eliminates diversification benefit - only a correlation of exactly +1.0 provides no diversification; anything below +1 reduces portfolio risk.", "Treating alpha as raw return - alpha is return in excess of the beta-adjusted (CAPM) expectation, not just outperformance versus the index.", "Assuming the mean is the 'typical' value in a skewed dataset - a few large outliers pull the mean away from the median."]},
  "econ-risk": {"summary": "Investment risk is the uncertainty that actual returns will differ from expected returns. The total risk of any security divides into two parts: systematic (market) risk, which affects the entire market and cannot be diversified away, and unsystematic (nonsystematic, or diversifiable) risk, which is specific to an individual company, industry, or sector and can be reduced by holding a well-diversified portfolio. Because investors can eliminate unsystematic risk through diversification, financial theory holds that the market only compensates investors for bearing systematic risk (measured by beta), not for taking on avoidable company-specific risk.\n\nSystematic risks are often remembered by the acronym PRIME: Purchasing-power (inflation) risk, Reinvestment risk, Interest-rate risk, Market risk, and Exchange-rate (currency) risk. Unsystematic risks are company/issuer-specific and include business risk, financial (credit/default) risk, liquidity/marketability risk, regulatory/legislative risk, and political/country risk. A key nuance: interest-rate risk is systematic and hits long-duration bonds hardest, while credit/default risk is unsystematic and can be diversified across many issuers.\n\nOpportunity cost is the value of the next-best alternative forgone when a choice is made; when the chosen option itself earns a return, the opportunity cost is the incremental (difference in) return given up relative to that choice. In finance it underlies the concept of the risk-free rate as a baseline required return: any investment should be expected to earn at least what a comparable-risk alternative (or the risk-free asset) would earn. Capital structure describes how a firm finances itself with a mix of debt and equity, and it drives the liquidation priority (the order of claims if the company is dissolved). Understanding both the risk each security carries and where it sits in the liquidation waterfall is central to matching a client's risk tolerance to suitable recommendations.", "keyPoints": ["Total risk = systematic risk + unsystematic risk; only unsystematic risk can be diversified away.", "Systematic (market) risk is non-diversifiable and is measured by beta; the market compensates investors only for systematic risk.", "Systematic risks (PRIME): Purchasing-power/inflation, Reinvestment, Interest-rate, Market, Exchange-rate (currency).", "Unsystematic risks are issuer-specific: business risk, financial/credit/default risk, liquidity/marketability risk, regulatory/legislative risk, political risk.", "Interest-rate risk is systematic and is greatest for long-maturity, low-coupon (long-duration) bonds.", "Reinvestment risk (systematic) is the risk that coupon/proceeds are reinvested at lower rates; zero-coupon bonds have NO reinvestment risk but the HIGHEST interest-rate risk.", "Purchasing-power (inflation) risk most harms long-term fixed-income holders; equities and real assets historically hedge it better.", "Credit/default risk is unsystematic and can be reduced by diversifying across many issuers; U.S. Treasuries are considered free of default risk but still carry interest-rate and inflation risk.", "Opportunity cost = the return given up from the next-best alternative, measured relative to the chosen option (the incremental return forgone when the chosen option also earns a return); the risk-free rate is the baseline benchmark.", "Capital structure = the firm's mix of long-term debt and equity used to finance operations.", "Liquidation priority (waterfall): (1) secured creditors, (2) unsecured creditors/general creditors incl. debentures & trade creditors, (3) subordinated debt, (4) preferred stockholders, (5) common stockholders last.", "Common stockholders are residual owners paid last in liquidation but hold unlimited upside and voting rights.", "Diversification reduces unsystematic risk but cannot reduce systematic risk below the market level.", "Debt (interest) is a legal obligation senior to equity; higher financial leverage increases financial risk to equity holders."], "formulas": ["Total risk = Systematic risk + Unsystematic risk", "Opportunity cost = Return on best forgone alternative − Return on chosen option (baseline benchmark = risk-free rate)", "Real (inflation-adjusted) return ≈ Nominal return − Inflation rate", "Beta measures systematic risk: portfolio return sensitivity relative to the market (market beta = 1.0)"], "mnemonics": ["PRIME = the systematic (non-diversifiable) risks: Purchasing-power, Reinvestment, Interest-rate, Market, Exchange-rate.", "Liquidation waterfall — creditors before owners: secured creditors → general/unsecured creditors (debentures, trade creditors) → subordinated debt → preferred stock → common stock (paid last).", "Systematic = 'System-wide' (whole market); Unsystematic = 'Unique' to one company."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing interest-rate risk (systematic) with credit/default risk (unsystematic) — they are different categories and only default risk is diversifiable.", "Assuming diversification lowers ALL risk — it only reduces unsystematic risk, never systematic/market risk.", "Thinking zero-coupon bonds are low-risk — they eliminate reinvestment risk but have the greatest interest-rate (price) risk for a given maturity.", "Believing U.S. Treasuries are 'risk-free' in every sense — they are free of default/credit risk but still carry interest-rate and purchasing-power risk.", "Placing preferred stock ahead of bondholders in liquidation — ALL debt (including subordinated debentures) is paid before ANY equity, and preferred is paid before common.", "Treating opportunity cost as an out-of-pocket expense — it is the forgone return on the alternative not chosen, not a cash outlay.", "Overstating opportunity cost as the full return of the alternative when your chosen option also earns something — the true opportunity cost is the incremental (difference in) return forgone."]},
  "veh-cash": {"summary": "Cash and cash equivalents are highly liquid, short-term, low-risk instruments that preserve principal and provide ready access to funds. On the Series 65, 'cash equivalents' generally means investments with an original maturity of one year or less (often 90 days or less), minimal credit risk, and minimal price/interest-rate risk. They fall into two families tested here: insured bank deposits, and money market instruments.\n\nInsured deposits are bank products covered by FDIC insurance up to $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank, per ownership category. This category includes demand deposits (checking accounts), savings and money market deposit accounts (MMDAs), and certificates of deposit (CDs). Retail CDs are time deposits that impose an early-withdrawal penalty; negotiable (jumbo) CDs carry a large minimum denomination (commonly $100,000 or more, typically trading in $1,000,000 blocks), trade in a secondary market, and are considered money market instruments, but FDIC coverage still caps at $250,000, so the full face of a large negotiable CD is not insured. Note that money market MUTUAL FUNDS are securities, not deposits, and carry no FDIC coverage.\n\nMoney market instruments are short-maturity debt sold largely at a discount. Treasury bills (T-bills) are direct obligations of the US government (no default risk), issued at a discount with no stated coupon; the discount is interest that is federally taxable but exempt from state and local tax. Commercial paper is unsecured, short-term corporate debt that is an exempt security under the Securities Act of 1933 when its maturity is 270 days or less and proceeds fund current operations. Other money market instruments include banker's acceptances (trade finance), repurchase agreements, and federal funds.", "keyPoints": ["FDIC insurance covers deposits (demand deposits, savings, MMDAs, and CDs) up to $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank, per ownership category.", "Demand deposits are checking accounts payable on demand with no maturity; since the repeal of Regulation Q (Dodd-Frank), they may pay interest.", "Retail CDs are time deposits with a fixed maturity and an early-withdrawal penalty; they are FDIC-insured within the limit.", "Negotiable (jumbo) CDs carry a large minimum denomination (commonly $100,000 or more, often trading in $1,000,000+ blocks) and trade in the secondary market (a money market instrument); FDIC coverage is still capped at $250,000, so the full face of a large CD is not insured.", "Money market mutual funds seek a stable $1.00 NAV but are securities and are NOT FDIC insured (unlike a bank money market deposit account).", "Money market instruments have maturities of one year or less and are typically issued at a discount.", "T-bills are backed by the full faith and credit of the US government (no default risk), issued at a discount with no coupon, and mature at par.", "T-bill (and all Treasury) interest is subject to federal income tax but exempt from state and local income tax.", "Commercial paper is unsecured, short-term corporate debt; it is an exempt security under the Securities Act of 1933 if its maturity is 270 days or less and it funds current transactions.", "Commercial paper carries default (credit) risk because it is unsecured; it is typically issued in large denominations to institutional buyers.", "Bank discount yields on T-bills and commercial paper are quoted using a 360-day year.", "'Risk-free' for T-bills refers to credit/default risk; they still have some interest-rate and reinvestment risk."], "formulas": ["Dollar discount = Face value - Purchase price", "T-bill dollar return (held to maturity) = Face value - Purchase price", "Bank discount yield = (Dollar discount / Face value) x (360 / days to maturity)"], "mnemonics": ["FDIC limit: '250 / depositor / bank / ownership category.'", "Commercial Paper = 'Corporate, unsecured, 270 days or less.'", "T-bills: 'Discount, no coupon, State-tax-free (federally taxable).'"], "pitfalls": ["Confusing a money market DEPOSIT account (FDIC-insured) with a money market MUTUAL FUND (not insured).", "Assuming a $1,000,000 negotiable CD is fully FDIC-insured — coverage stops at $250,000.", "Reversing T-bill taxation: they are federally taxable but state/local exempt, not the other way around.", "Using 270 days as the general money market maturity — 270 days is specifically the commercial paper exemption limit; money market maturities generally run up to one year.", "Thinking T-bills pay a coupon — they have no stated interest; the return is the discount.", "Forgetting that demand deposits may now pay interest after the repeal of Regulation Q."]},
  "veh-fixed-types": {"summary": "Fixed-income securities are debt obligations that promise a stream of interest and return of principal. On the Series 65, the tested distinctions are (1) who backs the bond, (2) how the interest is taxed, and (3) which risks dominate. U.S. Treasuries (bills, notes, bonds, STRIPS, TIPS) carry the full faith and credit of the U.S. government, so they are essentially free of default risk but still exposed to interest-rate risk (and, for fixed-coupon issues, purchasing-power/inflation risk). Treasury interest is taxed at the federal level but is exempt from state and local income tax. TIPS adjust their principal to the CPI, protecting against inflation, and STRIPS/TIPS generate 'phantom income' taxed annually before it is received.\n\nAgency and government-sponsored securities are a frequent trap. Ginnie Mae (GNMA) mortgage-backed pass-throughs carry the full faith and credit of the U.S. government, but Fannie Mae (FNMA) and Freddie Mac (FHLMC) are government-sponsored enterprises whose securities are NOT explicitly guaranteed. Mortgage-backed and asset-backed securities are pools of loans; they carry prepayment (call/reinvestment) risk when rates fall and extension risk when rates rise. Asset-backed securities (ABS) are similar structures collateralized by non-mortgage receivables (auto loans, credit cards, student loans, equipment leases) issued through a special purpose entity with credit enhancement.\n\nCorporate bonds are fully taxable (federal, state, and local) and pay higher yields to compensate for credit/default risk; secured debt (mortgage bonds, equipment trust certificates) ranks ahead of unsecured debentures, which rank ahead of subordinated debentures in liquidation. Municipal bonds pay interest that is generally exempt from federal income tax (and from state tax if the holder resides in the issuing state), making tax-equivalent yield the key comparison tool; general obligation (GO) bonds are backed by the issuer's full faith, credit, and taxing power, while revenue bonds are backed only by a specific project's revenues. Foreign bonds add currency (exchange-rate) risk and, for emerging markets, heightened political and sovereign-default risk.", "keyPoints": ["Treasury securities are backed by the full faith and credit of the U.S. government; interest is subject to federal tax but EXEMPT from state and local income tax.", "T-bills are zero-coupon: issued at a discount, mature at par, and pay no periodic interest. T-notes (2-10 yr) and T-bonds (20-30 yr) pay semiannual coupons.", "TIPS adjust principal for changes in the CPI; the fixed coupon rate is applied to the adjusted principal, so the dollar interest payment rises with inflation. At maturity the investor receives the greater of adjusted or original principal (deflation floor).", "STRIPS and TIPS produce 'phantom income' - imputed/inflation-adjustment income is taxed annually even though the cash is not received until maturity.", "Ginnie Mae (GNMA) pass-throughs ARE backed by full faith and credit of the U.S.; Fannie Mae (FNMA) and Freddie Mac (FHLMC) are GSEs and are NOT explicitly government-guaranteed.", "Mortgage-backed pass-throughs carry prepayment risk when rates fall (early principal, reinvestment risk) and extension risk when rates rise. Agency MBS interest is generally taxable at both federal and state levels (unlike Treasuries).", "Asset-backed securities (ABS) are pools of non-mortgage receivables (auto loans, credit card balances, student loans, equipment leases) securitized through a special purpose entity; interest is fully taxable.", "Corporate bond interest is fully taxable (federal, state, and local). Liquidation priority: secured creditors > senior unsecured debentures > subordinated debentures > preferred stock > common stock.", "Investment grade is rated BBB-/Baa3 and higher; below that is high-yield ('junk'), which pays more to compensate for greater default risk.", "Municipal bond interest is generally exempt from federal income tax and may be state-tax-exempt for in-state residents (potentially 'triple tax-free').", "General obligation (GO) bonds are backed by the issuer's full faith, credit, and taxing power (ad valorem/property taxes) and often need voter approval; revenue bonds are backed only by a specific project's revenues and generally do not require voter approval or fall under statutory debt limits.", "Insured municipal bonds carry a guaranty from a bond insurer, giving a higher rating and thus a LOWER yield than a comparable uninsured bond.", "Municipal bonds are generally unsuitable inside tax-advantaged accounts (IRAs) because the tax exemption is wasted; some private-activity municipal interest is subject to the AMT.", "Foreign bonds denominated in a foreign currency expose a U.S. investor to currency (exchange-rate) risk; a weakening foreign currency reduces the dollar value of interest and principal. Yankee bonds are foreign-issuer, U.S.-dollar-denominated bonds registered and sold in the U.S."], "formulas": ["Tax-equivalent yield = Municipal (tax-free) yield / (1 - marginal tax rate)", "After-tax yield on a taxable bond = Taxable yield x (1 - marginal tax rate)", "T-bill return (no coupon) = Par value - Purchase price (discount earned)", "TIPS interest payment = Fixed coupon rate x CPI-adjusted principal"], "mnemonics": ["'Ginnie is Government-guaranteed; Fannie and Freddie are just Friends (GSEs, not guaranteed).'", "'GO = Government's Own taxing power; REVenue = REVenue from the project only.'", "Liquidation priority order: 'Secured, Senior debentures, Subordinated, Preferred, Common.'", "Treasuries vs. municipals tax rule: 'Treasuries = Federal tax, no state; Munis = State-resident break, no federal.'"], "pitfalls": ["Assuming Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac carry the full faith and credit of the U.S. - only Ginnie Mae does.", "Thinking Treasury interest is tax-free - it is federally taxable; only STATE and LOCAL tax is exempt. Municipal interest is the reverse (federally exempt).", "Believing 'risk-free' Treasuries have no risk - they still carry interest-rate risk and (for fixed coupons) inflation/purchasing-power risk.", "Forgetting that TIPS and STRIPS create phantom income taxed annually before the cash is received.", "Assuming revenue bonds are backed by taxing power - they are backed only by project revenues, making them generally riskier than GO bonds.", "Thinking an insured muni yields more because it is safer - insurance raises the rating and LOWERS the yield.", "Overlooking prepayment/extension risk on mortgage- and asset-backed pass-throughs; they are not simple fixed-maturity bonds.", "Ignoring currency risk on foreign-currency-denominated bonds - even high-quality sovereign debt can lose value for a U.S. investor if the currency weakens."]},
  "veh-fixed-char": {"summary": "Fixed income securities are debt obligations in which an issuer borrows money and promises to pay periodic interest (the coupon) and repay principal (par/face value, usually $1,000) at maturity. Their market price moves inversely to interest rates and reflects the present value of all future cash flows discounted at the market's required yield. When the required yield equals the coupon, the bond trades at par; when the market yield rises above the coupon the bond trades at a discount; when the market yield falls below the coupon the bond trades at a premium. Key valuation inputs are time to maturity, coupon size, credit quality (ratings and the credit spread over Treasuries), and any embedded options such as call or conversion features.\n\nYield measures form a predictable hierarchy. For a discount bond: nominal (coupon) yield < current yield < YTM < YTC. For a premium bond the order reverses: nominal yield > current yield > YTM > YTC. Yield-to-maturity captures coupon income plus the movement of price toward par over the full life of the bond; yield-to-call assumes the issuer redeems at the first (or specified) call date, which matters most for premium/high-coupon bonds because issuers call when they can refinance cheaply. Duration measures interest-rate sensitivity: longer maturity, lower coupon, and lower yield all increase duration and therefore price volatility. A zero-coupon bond has duration equal to its maturity and the greatest volatility for a given maturity.\n\nTax treatment drives suitability. Corporate bond interest is fully taxable at federal and state levels; U.S. Treasury interest is federally taxable but state-tax exempt; municipal bond interest is federally tax-exempt (and state-exempt if in-state), which is why munis suit high-bracket investors and are usually inappropriate inside tax-deferred accounts. Zero-coupon bonds generate 'phantom income' — accreted OID taxed annually even though no cash is received — so they fit tax-deferred accounts or goal-date funding (college, retirement). In a liquidation, secured creditors are paid first, then unsecured/general creditors (including debentures), then subordinated debt, then preferred stock, and finally common stock; this liquidation preference is why bondholders sit ahead of equity holders.", "keyPoints": ["Bond prices move inversely to interest rates; price = present value of coupons + par discounted at the market's required yield.", "Trading relationship: coupon rate > market yield = premium; coupon < market yield = discount; coupon = market yield = par.", "Discount bond yield order (low to high): nominal < current < YTM < YTC; premium bond order reverses.", "YTC is the relevant return measure for premium/high-coupon callable bonds because issuers call to refinance when rates fall.", "Duration measures price sensitivity to rate changes; it rises with longer maturity, lower coupon, and lower yield.", "A zero-coupon bond's duration equals its maturity, giving it the most price volatility for a given maturity.", "Corporate interest is fully taxable; Treasury interest is federally taxable but state-exempt; municipal interest is federally tax-exempt.", "Zeros create phantom income (annual OID accretion taxed without cash), making them best held in tax-deferred accounts.", "Liquidation priority: secured creditors > general/unsecured creditors (debentures) > subordinated debt > preferred stock > common stock.", "Credit spread is the extra yield over a comparable-maturity Treasury; it widens when perceived default risk or economic stress rises.", "Investment grade is BBB-/Baa3 and above; BB+/Ba1 and below are high-yield ('junk') with wider spreads and higher default risk.", "Call features benefit the issuer, cap the bond's upside, and are exercised when rates fall; investors demand higher yields for call risk.", "A convertible bond's conversion value = (par / conversion price) x current stock price = conversion ratio x stock price.", "Callable bonds are typically priced to the yield-to-worst — the lower of YTM and YTC — to reflect the least favorable outcome to the investor."], "formulas": ["Current Yield = Annual Coupon Interest / Current Market Price", "Conversion Ratio = Par Value / Conversion Price", "Conversion (Parity) Value = Conversion Ratio x Current Stock Price", "Parity Price of Bond = Conversion Ratio x Market Price of Stock", "Credit Spread = Corporate (or muni) Bond Yield - Comparable-Maturity Treasury Yield", "Approx. price change % = -Modified Duration x change in yield (in decimal)", "Tax-Equivalent Yield = Municipal Yield / (1 - marginal tax rate)"], "mnemonics": ["'Premium PY, Discount DY' order — for a PREMIUM bond yields go Nominal > Current > YTM > YTC (high coupon on top); for a DISCOUNT bond the order flips low-to-high.", "'Longer, Lower, Lower = Longer duration' — longer maturity, lower coupon, lower yield all lengthen duration/volatility.", "Liquidation ladder 'Secured, General, Subordinated, Preferred, Common' — creditors before owners, senior before junior."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming higher coupon = higher total return; a premium bond amortizes toward par, so its YTM is LOWER than its current yield.", "Forgetting that issuers call bonds when rates FALL (to refinance cheaply), so investors face reinvestment risk exactly when reinvestment is least attractive.", "Recommending municipal bonds inside an IRA or 401(k) — the federal tax exemption is wasted in a tax-deferred account.", "Confusing a zero's lack of cash flow with lack of tax — OID accretion is taxed annually as phantom income.", "Thinking a higher bond rating means higher yield; higher-rated (lower-risk) bonds carry NARROWER spreads and LOWER yields.", "Assuming duration and maturity are the same; only a zero-coupon bond has duration equal to its maturity, coupon bonds have duration shorter than maturity.", "Pricing a callable premium bond off YTM; it should be evaluated at yield-to-worst, which for a premium callable bond is usually the YTC."]},
  "veh-equity-types": {"summary": "Equity securities represent ownership in a corporation. Common stock is the most basic ownership unit: it carries voting rights (typically one vote per share on directors and major corporate matters), the right to receive dividends when declared by the board, and a residual claim on assets in liquidation — meaning common shareholders are paid last, after creditors, bondholders, and preferred shareholders. Because their return depends on company performance and residual value, common stockholders bear the greatest risk but also enjoy the greatest potential for capital appreciation and unlimited upside. Ownership is limited to the amount invested (limited liability).\n\nForeign (non-U.S.) equity gives U.S. investors global diversification but adds currency (exchange-rate) risk, political/sovereign risk, and often lower transparency or different accounting standards. American Depositary Receipts (ADRs) are the common vehicle: a U.S. bank issues a negotiable receipt representing a specified number of shares of a foreign company held on deposit abroad. ADRs trade in U.S. dollars and pay dividends in dollars, but the holder still bears currency risk (the foreign dividend/price is converted to USD) and generally does NOT receive voting rights, which are held by the depositary bank. Foreign governments may also withhold tax on ADR dividends.\n\nPreferred stock is an equity security that behaves like a hybrid, with bond-like features. It typically pays a fixed, stated dividend (expressed as a percentage of par, usually $100, or a dollar amount) and has priority over common stock for both dividends and liquidation proceeds — but it still ranks behind all debt/creditors. Preferred is usually nonvoting and, because of its fixed dividend, its market price is sensitive to interest-rate changes (interest-rate risk), much like a bond. Preferred stock has limited appreciation potential because the dividend is fixed.\n\nSeveral preferred variants adjust these traits. Cumulative preferred requires that any skipped (omitted) dividends accumulate in arrears and be paid in full before common shareholders receive any dividend. Participating preferred can receive extra dividends beyond the stated rate. Callable preferred can be redeemed by the issuer, usually at par or a small premium, typically when rates fall. Convertible preferred can be exchanged by the holder for a fixed number of common shares (the conversion ratio); this links its price to the common stock, giving upside potential but usually carrying a lower stated dividend in exchange for that option. Convertibles trade at the higher of their investment value or their conversion (parity) value.", "keyPoints": ["Common stock: voting rights, dividends only when declared by the board, and a RESIDUAL claim on assets — paid last in liquidation, after creditors, bondholders, and preferred.", "Liquidation priority (highest to lowest): secured creditors, unsecured/general creditors (incl. bondholders), preferred stockholders, then common stockholders.", "Common stockholders have limited liability (max loss = amount invested) and the greatest growth potential/upside.", "ADRs represent foreign shares held on deposit at a U.S. bank; they trade and pay dividends in U.S. dollars but the holder still bears currency (exchange-rate) risk.", "ADR holders generally have NO voting rights (held by the depositary bank) and may face foreign dividend withholding tax.", "Foreign equity adds currency risk, political/sovereign risk, and reduced transparency versus domestic stock, in exchange for diversification.", "Preferred stock pays a FIXED dividend (stated as % of $100 par or a dollar amount), is usually nonvoting, and has priority over common for dividends and liquidation.", "Preferred stock has interest-rate risk: like a bond, its price falls when rates rise and rises when rates fall, because the dividend is fixed.", "Cumulative preferred: omitted dividends accumulate in arrears and must be paid in full before common receives any dividend; straight (noncumulative) preferred loses skipped dividends.", "Convertible preferred can be exchanged for a fixed number of common shares (conversion ratio); it usually carries a LOWER stated dividend in exchange for equity upside.", "Conversion (parity) value ties the convertible to the common: parity of preferred = market price of common x conversion ratio; a convertible sells at the greater of investment value or conversion value.", "Neither common nor preferred dividends are guaranteed — the board must declare them; preferred just has priority when they are paid."], "formulas": ["Conversion ratio = Par value of preferred / Conversion price", "Parity price of common stock = Market price of convertible preferred / Conversion ratio", "Parity price of convertible preferred = Market price of common x Conversion ratio", "Annual preferred dividend = Stated rate x Par ($100 unless stated otherwise); e.g., 6% preferred = $6/year"], "mnemonics": ["'CUMulative ACCUMulates' — skipped cumulative-preferred dividends pile up in arrears and get paid before common.", "Liquidation order 'Please Pay Bondholders, Preferred, then Peons (common)': creditors/Bonds -> Preferred -> Common (residual, last).", "ADR = 'A Dollar Receipt': trades and pays in USD, but currency risk and no vote remain."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming preferred dividends are guaranteed — they are not; the board must still declare them. Preferred only has PRIORITY over common.", "Thinking ADRs eliminate currency risk because they trade in dollars — the underlying foreign value is still converted to USD, so exchange-rate risk remains.", "Believing ADR holders vote like ordinary shareholders — voting rights are typically held by the depositary bank, not the ADR holder.", "Confusing cumulative with participating: cumulative = missed dividends accrue; participating = share in extra dividends above the stated rate.", "Forgetting that even cumulative preferred ranks BEHIND all creditors/bondholders in liquidation — priority is only relative to common.", "Assuming convertible preferred pays a higher dividend — it usually pays LESS than straight preferred because the holder is compensated with conversion (equity) upside."]},
  "veh-equity-char": {"summary": "Equity securities represent ownership. Common stockholders hold voting rights (electing directors and approving major corporate actions), a residual claim on earnings and assets, and the possibility of dividends and capital appreciation. In exchange for a fixed stated dividend and priority over common stock (for both dividends and liquidation), preferred stockholders normally give up voting rights. Two other ownership rights the exam tests are preemptive rights (the ability to buy a proportionate share of a new issue to avoid dilution) and the liquidation preference (preferred ranks ahead of common, but every stockholder ranks behind the company's creditors). In a dissolution the order is secured creditors, then general/unsecured creditors, then subordinated debt, then preferred stock, and common stock last.\n\nDividends must be declared by the board of directors and are never guaranteed. Cumulative preferred stock is a favorite calculation topic: any skipped ('in arrears') dividends accumulate and must all be paid, together with the current year, before common stockholders receive anything. Noncumulative preferred does not make up skipped dividends. Candidates should also know the sequence of dividend dates (declaration, ex-dividend, record, payable) and that an investor must own the shares before the ex-dividend date to receive the payment.\n\nRestricted stock is unregistered stock (typically acquired in a Reg D private placement); control stock is stock held by an affiliate. SEC Rule 144 governs resale into the public market. The holding period is six months for a reporting issuer and one year for a non-reporting issuer. Affiliates remain subject to a volume cap each 90 days equal to the greater of 1% of shares outstanding or the average weekly trading volume of the prior four weeks, and must file Form 144 when a sale exceeds 5,000 shares or $50,000 in a three-month period.\n\nEmployee stock options come in two flavors. Nonqualified stock options (NSOs) create ordinary compensation income equal to the bargain element (FMV minus exercise price) at exercise, and the employer gets a matching deduction. Incentive (statutory) stock options (ISOs) produce no regular tax at grant or exercise, but the bargain element at exercise is an alternative minimum tax (AMT) adjustment; if the employee holds the stock at least two years from grant and one year from exercise, the entire gain is long-term capital gain and the employer takes no deduction.", "keyPoints": ["Common stock conveys voting rights, a residual claim, and possible (never guaranteed) dividends; it is paid last in a liquidation.", "Typical preferred stock is nonvoting but carries a fixed stated dividend and priority over common for dividends and in liquidation.", "Cumulative preferred accumulates skipped dividends (arrears) that must be paid, with the current year's, before any common dividend; noncumulative preferred does not make up skipped dividends.", "Preemptive rights let existing holders buy a proportionate part of a new issue to avoid dilution; antidilution provisions protect an owner's percentage stake.", "Liquidation priority: secured creditors, then general/unsecured creditors (incl. bondholders), then subordinated debt, then preferred, then common last.", "A liquidation preference ranks preferred ahead of common but always behind creditors; it does not guarantee ongoing dividends.", "Restricted stock is unregistered (e.g., Reg D private placement); control stock is held by an affiliate; Rule 144 governs public resale.", "Rule 144 holding period is 6 months for an SEC-reporting issuer and 1 year for a non-reporting issuer.", "Rule 144 affiliate 90-day volume cap = greater of 1% of shares outstanding or average weekly volume over the prior 4 weeks; Form 144 is filed when a sale exceeds 5,000 shares or $50,000 in 90 days.", "Non-affiliates are not subject to the Rule 144 volume cap once the holding period is met and may sell freely after one year.", "Dividends must be declared by the board; an investor must own the shares before the ex-dividend date to receive the dividend.", "NSO: bargain element (FMV - exercise price) is ordinary income at exercise and the employer gets a deduction; NSOs can be granted to non-employees.", "ISO: no regular tax at grant or exercise, but the bargain element at exercise is an AMT adjustment; must be an employee; hold 2 years from grant and 1 year from exercise for all-capital-gain treatment and the employer gets no deduction on a qualifying disposition."], "formulas": ["Annual preferred dividend = par value x stated dividend rate.", "Cumulative preferred owed before common = annual dividend x (years in arrears + current year).", "Rule 144 affiliate 90-day sale limit = greater of (1% x shares outstanding) or (average weekly trading volume over preceding 4 weeks).", "Option bargain element = fair market value at exercise - exercise (strike) price."], "mnemonics": ["ISO = 'I Sit On it': hold 2 years from grant and 1 year from exercise for long-term capital gains; no ordinary income at exercise but watch AMT.", "Liquidation line-up 'Secured, Unsecured, Subordinated, Preferred, Plain-common': creditors always before owners, common dead last.", "NSO = 'Now the Salary is Ordinary' - taxed as ordinary income at exercise, with an employer deduction."], "pitfalls": ["Swapping ISO and NSO tax: NSOs create ordinary income (and an employer deduction) at exercise; ISOs create none for regular tax but the bargain element is an AMT item.", "Forgetting to add the current year to the arrears when computing cumulative preferred owed; or assuming noncumulative preferred must make up skipped dividends (it does not).", "Applying the Rule 144 volume cap to non-affiliates - the cap and Form 144 requirement apply to affiliates; and mixing up the 6-month (reporting) vs 1-year (non-reporting) holding periods.", "Assuming preferred stock votes or that any dividend is guaranteed - dividends require board declaration, and preferred is usually nonvoting.", "Believing a liquidation preference puts preferred ahead of creditors; every stockholder, preferred included, is paid only after all creditors."]},
  "veh-equity-value": {"summary": "Equity valuation on the Series 65 falls into two broad camps. Technical analysis studies price and volume history through charts to time trades, assuming that market psychology and supply/demand patterns repeat. Fundamental analysis studies the underlying business and economy to estimate what a security is intrinsically worth, then compares that value to the market price. Fundamental analysts typically work top-down (economy, then industry, then company) and rely on financial statements and ratios such as P/E, P/B, dividend yield, and EPS.\n\nWithin fundamental analysis, two present-value models are heavily tested. The dividend discount model (DDM) values a stock as the present value of all expected future dividends. The constant-growth (Gordon Growth) version divides next year's dividend by the required return minus the dividend growth rate. The discounted cash flow (DCF) approach is the more general idea: value equals the sum of expected future cash flows discounted to the present at an appropriate required rate of return; DDM is simply a DCF that uses dividends as the cash flow.\n\nA key exam theme is the inverse relationship between required return (the discount rate) and value: raising the discount rate lowers present value, and lowering it raises present value. Another theme is distinguishing the tools of the technician (charts, trendlines, moving averages, support/resistance, breadth, and sentiment indicators) from the tools of the fundamentalist (statements, ratios, growth rates, and discounting). The efficient market hypothesis is often paired with these topics: strong-form efficiency implies neither technical nor fundamental analysis can consistently beat the market, while weak-form efficiency specifically undercuts technical analysis because past prices are already reflected in current prices.", "keyPoints": ["Technical analysis forecasts price using historical price and volume (charts, trends, patterns); it ignores intrinsic value and focuses on timing and market psychology.", "Support is a price floor where buying tends to emerge; resistance is a price ceiling where selling tends to emerge. A breakout occurs when price pierces either level.", "Common technical tools include trendlines, moving averages, chart patterns (head-and-shoulders), market breadth (advance/decline), short interest, and sentiment indicators.", "Fundamental analysis estimates intrinsic value from the economy, industry, and company financials, then compares it to market price to find over- or under-valuation.", "Top-down fundamental analysis moves economy to industry to company; key ratios include P/E, P/B, dividend yield, current ratio, and debt-to-equity.", "A stock trading below its estimated intrinsic value is considered undervalued (a potential buy); above intrinsic value it is overvalued.", "The dividend discount model (DDM) values a stock as the present value of all expected future dividends.", "Constant-growth DDM (Gordon Growth): Value = D1 / (k - g), where D1 is next year's dividend, k is required return, and g is the constant dividend growth rate. It requires k > g.", "In the DDM, D1 = D0 x (1 + g); use next year's expected dividend in the numerator, not the current dividend.", "Discounted cash flow (DCF) values an asset as the present value of its expected future cash flows discounted at the required rate of return; DDM is a special case of DCF using dividends.", "The discount rate and present value move inversely: a higher required return lowers value; a lower required return raises value.", "Weak-form efficient market hypothesis implies technical analysis cannot consistently add value because past prices are already reflected in current prices; strong-form implies neither technical nor fundamental analysis works consistently."], "formulas": ["Constant-growth DDM (Gordon Growth): V = D1 / (k - g), valid only when k > g", "Next dividend: D1 = D0 x (1 + g)", "Dividend yield = Annual dividend / Current price", "P/E ratio = Market price per share / Earnings per share", "DCF (general): Value = sum of [CFt / (1 + k)^t] for t = 1 to n"], "mnemonics": ["Technicals = 'Timing & charts'; Fundamentals = 'Firm & finances'.", "DDM numerator is D-ONE (next year), not D-zero.", "Discount rate UP, value DOWN (they seesaw)."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing the two schools: charts, volume, support/resistance, and moving averages are technical; ratios, statements, and discounting are fundamental.", "Putting the current dividend (D0) in the Gordon Growth numerator instead of next year's dividend (D1 = D0 x (1+g)).", "Forgetting that constant-growth DDM only works when the required return k exceeds the growth rate g.", "Thinking a higher discount rate raises value; the relationship is inverse.", "Assuming DDM and DCF are unrelated; DDM is just a DCF that uses dividends as the cash flow.", "Believing technical analysis identifies 'intrinsic value' - it does not; only fundamental analysis estimates intrinsic worth.", "Misreading undervalued vs overvalued: intrinsic value ABOVE market price means undervalued (buy candidate)."]},
  "veh-offering": {"summary": "An equity public offering is the sale of stock to the investing public under the Securities Act of 1933, which requires registration (a filed registration statement plus a prospectus) unless an exemption applies. The two core primary-market events are the initial public offering (IPO)—the first time a company sells shares to the public and becomes publicly traded—and a subsequent (follow-on) primary offering. Both are underwritten distributions of newly issued shares in which the issuer receives the sale proceeds (net of the underwriting spread). Registration under the 1933 Act is about full and fair DISCLOSURE; the SEC never approves the offering or passes on the merits, and saying otherwise is a violation.\n\nThe term 'secondary offering' is used two ways, and the Series 65 tests the distinction. A true secondary offering (or the secondary portion of a distribution) is a registered sale of ALREADY-OUTSTANDING shares by large existing holders (founders, early investors, insiders); the SELLING SHAREHOLDERS receive the proceeds, not the issuer, so there is no new capital raised and no dilution of share count. This is distinct from 'secondary market' trading (ordinary investor-to-investor exchange/OTC trading) and from a follow-on PRIMARY offering (new shares, issuer gets the money). A combined offering that sells both new issuer shares and existing holders' shares is a split (or combined) offering.\n\nA SPAC (special purpose acquisition company)—also called a blank-check or blind-pool company—IPOs as a shell with no operating business. It raises cash into a trust with the plan of finding and acquiring a private company (the 'de-SPAC') within a set window, typically about 24 months. Investors buy in without knowing the eventual target (hence 'blind pool' / 'blank check'), so disclosure of that risk is central. If no deal closes in time, the trust is liquidated and public shareholders get their money back (usually near the IPO price plus interest). Because of the heightened risk of blank-check securities, they carry special regulatory treatment (e.g., SEC Rule 419 for blank-check companies that issue penny stock, and enhanced NASAA/state scrutiny), and an adviser recommending one must reasonably believe it suits the client.", "keyPoints": ["The Securities Act of 1933 governs the primary market (new issues); it requires registration and delivery of a prospectus, and its purpose is DISCLOSURE, not merit review — the SEC never approves an offering or vouches for it.", "In an IPO, a private company sells shares to the public for the first time and becomes a reporting/publicly traded company; it is a PRIMARY offering where the issuer receives the proceeds.", "A follow-on (additional) PRIMARY offering sells newly issued shares after the IPO — the issuer gets the money and existing shareholders' ownership is diluted because the share count increases.", "A true SECONDARY offering is a registered resale of already-outstanding shares by existing large holders/insiders; the SELLING SHAREHOLDERS receive the proceeds, the issuer gets nothing, and total shares outstanding do not change (no dilution).", "Do not confuse a 'secondary offering' (registered distribution of outstanding stock) with the 'secondary market' (ongoing investor-to-investor trading on exchanges/OTC) — different concepts.", "A combined/split offering distributes both newly issued (primary) shares and existing (secondary) shares in the same registered deal.", "The underwriting spread is the difference between the public offering price and the amount the issuer/seller receives; it is the underwriters' compensation.", "A SPAC (blank-check / blind-pool company) IPOs as a shell with no operations, raising cash to acquire an as-yet-unidentified target within a set window (commonly ~24 months).", "'Blind pool' means investors commit money before knowing what will be bought; 'blank check' means the shell has broad discretion over the eventual target — both describe the SPAC structure and highlight disclosure risk.", "If a SPAC fails to complete its acquisition (de-SPAC) in the allowed time, the trust is liquidated and public investors typically receive their pro-rata cash back, near the IPO price plus interest.", "Blank-check companies that issue penny stock receive heightened regulatory treatment (e.g., SEC Rule 419), and NASAA/state regulators scrutinize blind pools closely because of investor-protection concerns.", "For any equity offering, an IAR must have a reasonable basis that the security is suitable for the specific client; the speculative nature of IPOs and SPACs makes suitability especially important."], "formulas": ["Underwriting spread = Public offering price (POP) - Proceeds to issuer/selling shareholder (per share)", "New shares issued in a primary offering => share count increases => existing holders are diluted; a pure secondary offering leaves share count unchanged (no dilution)"], "mnemonics": ["Primary = the comPany gets the Proceeds (new shares); Secondary = the Sellers (existing Shareholders) get the money (old shares).", "SPAC red flags: Shell, Pool (blind), Acquisition (unidentified), Cash-in-trust.", "Secondary OFFERING (registered distribution of outstanding shares) vs secondary MARKET (investor-to-investor trading) — 'offering' involves a prospectus; 'market' does not."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming any offering after the IPO is a 'secondary offering' — a follow-on sale of NEW shares is a follow-on PRIMARY offering (issuer gets money, dilution occurs); only resales of OUTSTANDING shares are true secondaries.", "Confusing the 'secondary market' (ongoing trading) with a 'secondary offering' (a registered distribution of existing shares).", "Believing SEC registration/effectiveness means the SEC approved or endorsed the security — registration is disclosure only, and claiming SEC approval is unlawful.", "Thinking a secondary offering dilutes existing shareholders — it does not, because no new shares are created; only primary offerings dilute.", "Assuming a SPAC's IPO proceeds are freely usable by management — they are held in trust and returned to investors if no qualifying acquisition closes in time.", "Treating a blind-pool/blank-check IPO as low-risk because it holds cash — it is a speculative shell with no operating history and an unknown target, requiring careful suitability analysis."]},
  "veh-pooled-types": {"summary": "Pooled investments gather money from many investors into a single portfolio managed toward a stated objective. The Investment Company Act of 1940 defines three types of registered investment companies: open-end management companies (mutual funds), closed-end management companies (closed-end funds), and unit investment trusts (UITs). Mutual funds continuously issue and redeem shares at net asset value (NAV) computed at least daily; closed-end funds issue a fixed number of shares that then trade on an exchange at a market price that can be at a premium or discount to NAV. UITs hold a fixed, unmanaged portfolio, have a stated termination date, use no board of directors or investment adviser, and issue redeemable units.\n\nETFs are typically registered as open-end funds (or occasionally UITs) but trade intraday on an exchange like a stock; an in-kind creation/redemption mechanism using authorized participants keeps market price close to NAV and enhances tax efficiency. REITs are pooled real-estate vehicles that avoid corporate-level tax by distributing at least 90% of taxable income to shareholders; listed REITs trade on exchanges and are liquid, while non-traded (non-listed) REITs are illiquid, carry high upfront commissions/fees, and often rely on stale, appraisal-based valuations.\n\nPrivate funds (hedge funds, private equity, venture capital) rely on exemptions from Investment Company Act registration (Sections 3(c)(1) and 3(c)(7)) and sell through private placements under Regulation D to accredited investors and qualified purchasers. They are illiquid, use lock-ups and redemption gates, charge performance-based fees (commonly '2 and 20'), may use leverage and short selling, and are not subject to the same disclosure, liquidity, and diversification rules as registered funds.", "keyPoints": ["The Investment Company Act of 1940 recognizes three investment company types: open-end (mutual fund), closed-end, and unit investment trust (UIT).", "Mutual fund (open-end) shares are continuously offered and redeemable; they are priced at NAV (forward pricing) computed at least once per business day, and do not trade on an exchange.", "Closed-end funds issue a fixed number of shares in an IPO that then trade on a secondary market at a market price which may be above (premium) or below (discount) NAV based on supply and demand.", "A UIT holds a fixed, unmanaged (not actively traded) portfolio, has no board of directors and no investment adviser, has a set termination/maturity date, and issues redeemable units.", "REITs must distribute at least 90% of taxable income to shareholders to qualify for pass-through (conduit) tax treatment and avoid corporate-level income tax; most income dividends are ordinary (not qualified) and lack the dividends-received deduction.", "Listed REITs trade on exchanges and are liquid; non-traded REITs are illiquid, have high upfront sales loads/fees, limited redemption programs, and NAVs based on periodic appraisals rather than daily market prices.", "REITs invest in real estate/mortgages and are NOT investment companies; equity REITs own property and earn rents, mortgage REITs lend and earn interest income.", "ETFs trade intraday at market prices; authorized participants create/redeem large blocks (creation units) in-kind, which arbitrages price toward NAV and improves tax efficiency by minimizing capital-gains distributions.", "Private funds avoid Investment Company Act registration via Section 3(c)(1) (no more than 100 beneficial owners) or 3(c)(7) (only qualified purchasers), and sell via Regulation D private placements.", "Hedge funds, private equity, and venture capital are illiquid, use lock-up periods and redemption gates, and typically charge a performance fee (carried interest) plus a management fee (e.g., '2 and 20').", "Accredited investor thresholds (individual): $200,000 income ($300,000 joint) in each of the two most recent years with expectation of the same, OR $1,000,000 net worth excluding primary residence.", "Mutual fund share classes differ by sales-charge structure: Class A (front-end load, breakpoints), Class B (back-end/contingent deferred sales charge that declines over time), Class C (level load, higher ongoing 12b-1 fees).", "NAV per share = (Total assets - Total liabilities) / Number of shares outstanding; a mutual fund's public offering price = NAV + any front-end sales charge.", "ETFs can generally be sold short and bought on margin and may pay dividends; UITs and mutual funds cannot be traded intraday."], "formulas": ["NAV per share = (Total Assets - Total Liabilities) / Shares Outstanding", "Mutual fund Public Offering Price (POP) = NAV / (1 - Sales Charge %)", "Sales Charge % = (POP - NAV) / POP", "REIT qualification: distribute >= 90% of taxable income to owners for conduit tax treatment", "'2 and 20' hedge fund fee = 2% annual management fee on assets + 20% performance fee (carried interest) on profits"], "mnemonics": ["OPEN-end = OPEN-ended shares (unlimited, redeemable at NAV); CLOSED-end = CLOSED share count (fixed, trades on exchange).", "UIT = 'Unmanaged, Isn't traded (actively), Terminates' - no adviser, fixed portfolio, set end date.", "REIT '90/75/75': distribute 90% of income; 75% of assets in real estate; 75% of gross income from real estate.", "Hedge fund gatekeepers: 3(c)(1) = 100 or fewer owners; 3(c)(7) = only Qualified purchasers (Club of $5M+)."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing closed-end funds with open-end funds: only closed-end funds trade at a market price that can differ from NAV (premium/discount); open-end shares always transact at NAV (plus any load).", "Assuming ETFs are always priced exactly at NAV - they trade at market prices intraday and can briefly deviate from NAV; the arbitrage mechanism keeps them close, it does not guarantee equality.", "Thinking a UIT is actively managed or has an investment adviser/board - it has neither; the portfolio is fixed until termination.", "Believing REIT dividends are 'qualified dividends' - most REIT income distributions are taxed as ordinary income and are not eligible for the qualified-dividend rate or the corporate dividends-received deduction.", "Assuming REITs are investment companies - they are not registered under the Investment Company Act of 1940; do not confuse a REIT with a real estate limited partnership (a REIT does not pass through losses).", "Treating non-traded REITs as liquid because they are 'REITs' - they are illiquid with high fees, limited redemptions, and appraisal-based valuations, unlike exchange-listed REITs.", "Confusing accredited investor with qualified purchaser: 3(c)(7) funds require qualified purchasers (generally $5M+ in investments), a much higher bar than accredited investor status.", "Assuming all private-fund advisers are exempt from registration - the private fund adviser and venture capital adviser exemptions are limited; many must still register or file as exempt reporting advisers."]},
  "veh-pooled-char": {"summary": "Pooled investments combine money from many investors into a single portfolio managed toward a stated objective. The dominant vehicle is the open-end management company (mutual fund), which continuously issues and redeems its own redeemable shares directly with the fund at net asset value (NAV) computed once per business day using forward pricing. Because open-end funds redeem daily at NAV, they are highly liquid, but they never trade at a discount or premium. Closed-end funds, by contrast, issue a fixed number of shares in an IPO that then trade intraday on an exchange at a market price set by supply and demand, which can be at a premium or (more commonly) a discount to NAV. ETFs also trade intraday on exchanges but use a creation/redemption arbitrage mechanism that keeps market price close to NAV, and they are typically low-cost and tax-efficient.\n\nMutual funds sell different share classes representing the same portfolio but with different sales-charge and expense arrangements. Class A shares carry a front-end load but offer breakpoints (volume discounts) and lower ongoing 12b-1 fees; Class B shares use a contingent deferred sales charge (CDSC) that declines to zero over time and typically convert to Class A; Class C shares (\"level load\") have little or no front-end load, a short 1-year CDSC, and high ongoing 12b-1 fees, making them costly for long holding periods. FINRA caps 12b-1 fees at 0.75% (plus up to 0.25% service fee) and a fund may not call itself \"no-load\" if its 12b-1/service fees exceed 0.25%.\n\nTaxation is driven by pass-through (conduit/Subchapter M) treatment: to qualify as a regulated investment company (RIC), a fund must distribute at least 90% of its net investment income; once qualified, it is taxed only on any amounts it retains, and distributions retain their character in the shareholder's hands (to avoid essentially all fund-level tax, a fund distributes substantially all of its income and gains). Dividends and capital gains distributions are taxable to the investor in the year received even if reinvested, and long-term capital gain distributions are taxed at long-term rates based on how long the FUND held the securities, regardless of how long the investor owned the fund shares. Comparing funds requires looking past raw return to expense ratio, the appropriate benchmark/index, manager tenure, turnover, and risk.", "keyPoints": ["Open-end (mutual) funds continuously issue and redeem redeemable shares at NAV; closed-end funds issue a fixed share count that trades on an exchange at market price.", "NAV per share = (total assets - total liabilities) / shares outstanding, computed at least once each business day.", "Mutual funds use forward pricing: purchase/redemption orders receive the next computed NAV, not the last posted NAV.", "Only closed-end funds and ETFs can trade at a premium or discount to NAV; open-end fund shares are always transacted at NAV (plus any applicable load).", "Class A = front-end load with breakpoints and low 12b-1; Class B = declining CDSC that converts to A; Class C = level load, high ongoing 12b-1, small short-term CDSC.", "FINRA caps asset-based 12b-1 distribution fees at 0.75% of average net assets annually, plus up to a 0.25% service fee (1.00% combined ceiling).", "A fund cannot be marketed as 'no-load' if 12b-1 plus service fees exceed 0.25% of average net assets per year.", "Breakpoints are volume discounts on Class A front-end loads; a letter of intent (13 months, retroactive) and rights of accumulation help investors reach them.", "A 'breakpoint sale' - recommending a purchase just below a breakpoint to keep a higher commission - is a prohibited unethical practice.", "Under Subchapter M (conduit/pipeline theory), a RIC must distribute at least 90% of net investment income to qualify; it is then taxed only on retained amounts, and distributions keep their character.", "Reinvested dividends and capital-gains distributions are taxable in the year received; reinvestment does not defer tax.", "Long-term capital gain distributions are taxed at LT rates based on the fund's holding period of the securities, regardless of how long the investor held the fund.", "Mutual funds are highly liquid (daily redemption at NAV); closed-end fund liquidity depends on secondary-market trading volume.", "When comparing funds, weigh expense ratio, turnover, manager tenure, and returns against the correct benchmark index - not raw return alone."], "formulas": ["NAV per share = (Total assets - Total liabilities) / Number of shares outstanding", "Public Offering Price (POP) for a load fund = NAV / (1 - sales charge %)", "Sales charge % = (POP - NAV) / POP", "Expense ratio = Annual fund operating expenses / Average net assets", "Premium/Discount % = (Market price - NAV) / NAV"], "mnemonics": ["ABC classes: A = front (load Ahead), B = Back-end/CDSC, C = Continuous level load (Costly long-term).", "'Forward pricing' = you get the FUTURE (next-calculated) NAV, never the stale one posted.", "Conduit/pipeline: income 'flows through' the fund to the investor - distribute 90%+ of net investment income to qualify as a RIC and escape fund-level tax on the amounts passed through."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming open-end funds can trade at a premium/discount - they cannot; only closed-end funds and ETFs do.", "Thinking Class B or Class C shares are 'cheaper' because there is no front-end load - high 12b-1 fees make C shares expensive for long holders and B shares carry a CDSC.", "Believing reinvested distributions are tax-deferred - they are taxable in the year received.", "Confusing the investor's holding period with the fund's - LT capital gain distribution character is set by how long the fund held the security.", "Calling any fund with a 0.25% or lower 12b-1 'load' - a fund can still be 'no-load' up to 0.25%; above that it cannot.", "Ignoring that a 'level load' Class C still has a small (usually 1%) CDSC in the first year.", "Comparing a fund's return to the wrong benchmark (e.g., a small-cap fund vs. the S&P 500) and drawing false conclusions about manager skill."]},
  "veh-deriv-types": {"summary": "Derivative securities are financial instruments whose value is derived from an underlying asset, index, or reference rate rather than from any intrinsic worth of their own. The Series 65 exam expects candidates to recognize the four core derivative types and their defining mechanics: options, warrants, futures, and forward contracts. The central distinctions are (1) whether the holder has a RIGHT versus an OBLIGATION to transact, and (2) whether the contract is exchange-traded and standardized versus privately negotiated.\n\nOptions give the buyer (holder) the RIGHT, but not the obligation, to buy (call) or sell (put) an underlying asset at a fixed strike price before or at expiration; the seller (writer) receives a premium and takes on the obligation if exercised. Warrants are long-term instruments, typically issued by a corporation (often attached to a bond or preferred stock as a 'sweetener'), giving the holder the right to buy that issuer's stock at a set price. Because a warrant is exercised against the issuer, it results in newly issued shares and dilution, whereas a listed call option is written by another market participant and creates no new shares.\n\nFutures and forwards both obligate BOTH parties to transact at a set price on a future date. The key difference is standardization and venue: futures are exchange-traded, standardized, marked-to-market daily, and backed by a clearinghouse, so counterparty (default) risk is minimal. Forwards are private, customizable, over-the-counter agreements settled at maturity, carrying greater counterparty risk and lower liquidity. Unlike an option, a futures or forward holder cannot simply walk away by letting the contract lapse; the obligation binds unless the position is offset or delivery occurs.", "keyPoints": ["A derivative's value is derived from an underlying asset, index, or rate; it has no independent value of its own apart from that reference.", "An option gives the HOLDER a right (not an obligation); the WRITER (seller) has the obligation and collects the premium.", "A CALL gives the right to BUY the underlying at the strike; a PUT gives the right to SELL at the strike.", "Warrants are long-term equity rights (often years) issued BY the corporation, frequently attached to bonds/preferred as a sweetener.", "Exercising a warrant produces newly issued shares and causes dilution; exercising a listed call does not create new shares.", "Warrants are typically issued with a strike price ABOVE the current market price and have long lives; standard listed options are short-term (often <= 9 months, LEAPS longer).", "Futures and forwards OBLIGATE both the buyer and the seller to transact; there is no right to simply let them expire unexercised.", "Futures are exchange-traded, standardized, and marked-to-market daily with a clearinghouse as counterparty, minimizing default risk.", "Forward contracts are private, customizable OTC agreements settled at maturity, with higher counterparty (credit) risk and low liquidity.", "Buying an option caps the buyer's maximum loss at the premium paid; a futures/forward position can produce losses far exceeding any initial margin.", "Options and futures are traded on exchanges; forwards are not exchange-traded.", "A warrant holder receives no dividends and has no voting rights until the warrant is exercised into stock."], "formulas": ["Call intrinsic value = Market price of underlying - Strike price (floored at 0)", "Put intrinsic value = Strike price - Market price of underlying (floored at 0)", "Option premium = Intrinsic value + Time value"], "mnemonics": ["Call Up, Put Down: a call is bullish (profits as price rises), a put is bearish (profits as price falls).", "RIGHTs are for Options/Warrants; OBLIGATIONs bind both parties in Futures/Forwards.", "'Forward = Foreign/OTC, Future = Formalized (exchange)' to remember which is standardized."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing a warrant with a call option: warrants are issuer-created (dilutive, long-term); listed calls are written by market participants (non-dilutive, short-term).", "Thinking a futures holder can 'let it expire' like an option; futures obligate both sides to perform unless the position is offset.", "Assuming forwards are safer than futures; forwards actually carry MORE counterparty risk because there is no clearinghouse and no daily mark-to-market.", "Believing the option WRITER has the choice to exercise; only the HOLDER exercises, and the writer must perform if assigned.", "Mixing up call and put direction, or assuming intrinsic value can be negative (it is floored at zero)."]},
  "veh-deriv-char": {"summary": "Derivatives are securities whose value is derived from an underlying asset, index, rate, or benchmark. The two most heavily tested on the Series 65 are options (contracts giving the right to buy or sell) and forwards/futures (obligations to buy or sell at a set price on a future date). They are used for three broad purposes: hedging (reducing risk on an existing position), speculation (making a leveraged bet on price direction), and income generation (e.g., writing covered calls). Because a small premium or margin deposit controls a much larger position, derivatives embody leverage — magnifying both gains and losses relative to the capital committed.\n\nThe headline benefit is efficient risk transfer at low cost: a protective put insures a stock position for the price of a premium, and a hedger can lock in a price to remove uncertainty. The headline risk is that leverage cuts both ways and many positions carry limited life and time decay. Option buyers face limited, defined loss (the premium paid) but can lose 100% of it, and their contracts expire. Option writers (sellers) collect a premium but take on much larger, sometimes unlimited, risk — an uncovered (naked) call writer has theoretically unlimited loss because a stock's price has no ceiling. Futures obligate both parties, so a long or short futures position carries substantial and potentially unlimited loss with only a margin deposit at stake.\n\nBeyond market risk and leverage, derivatives add counterparty (credit) risk, especially for over-the-counter (OTC) instruments like forwards and swaps that are privately negotiated and not guaranteed by a clearinghouse. Exchange-traded, standardized contracts (listed options, futures) reduce counterparty risk through a clearinghouse (the OCC for listed options) but sacrifice customization and may carry liquidity risk in thinly traded series. Complexity, valuation difficulty, and time-sensitivity make derivatives generally unsuitable for unsophisticated clients or as a core holding — an important suitability point for IARs.", "keyPoints": ["A derivative's value is derived from an underlying asset, index, rate, or reference — it is not an ownership claim in itself.", "Leverage is the defining feature: a small premium or margin deposit controls a much larger notional position, magnifying both gains and losses.", "Three main uses tested: hedging (risk reduction), speculation (leveraged directional bet), and income (e.g., writing covered calls).", "Option BUYERS have limited risk (the premium paid) but can lose 100% of it, and the contract can expire worthless.", "Option WRITERS (sellers) receive a premium but assume much larger risk; an uncovered (naked) call writer has theoretically UNLIMITED loss because a stock price has no upper limit.", "A protective (long) put hedges a long stock position — it functions like insurance, capping downside for the cost of the premium while preserving upside.", "Futures/forwards OBLIGATE both parties (no choice to walk away), so both long and short carry substantial, potentially unlimited loss with only margin posted.", "OTC derivatives (forwards, swaps) carry counterparty/credit risk because they are privately negotiated and not clearinghouse-guaranteed.", "Exchange-traded listed options are standardized and guaranteed by the OCC (Options Clearing Corporation), which sharply reduces counterparty risk.", "Options and futures have limited lives; long options suffer time decay (theta) — value erodes as expiration nears, all else equal.", "Because of leverage, complexity, and time-sensitivity, derivatives are generally unsuitable as a core holding for conservative or unsophisticated clients.", "A covered call generates income and offers limited downside cushion (the premium) but CAPS upside at the strike price plus premium received."], "formulas": ["Long call breakeven = strike price + premium paid", "Long put breakeven = strike price - premium paid", "Long call max loss = premium paid; Long put max loss = premium paid", "Covered call max gain = (strike - stock cost) + premium; downside cushion = premium received", "Naked call max loss = unlimited (stock price has no ceiling)"], "mnemonics": ["Buyers = Bounded loss (premium), Rights, and expiration; Writers = Wider (larger/unlimited) risk for premium income.", "CALL UP / PUT DOWN: call buyers profit when the underlying rises, put buyers profit when it falls.", "'Insurance = protective put': you pay a premium to protect what you own."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing option buyers with writers: the BUYER's loss is limited to the premium; the naked-call WRITER faces unlimited loss.", "Assuming futures have limited loss like a long option — futures obligate both sides, so loss can be far larger than the margin deposit.", "Thinking a covered call is high-risk — it is an income/limited-risk strategy; the real cost is giving up (capping) upside above the strike.", "Believing all derivatives eliminate counterparty risk — only clearinghouse-guaranteed exchange products (e.g., OCC-cleared options) do; OTC forwards/swaps do not.", "Treating leverage as purely a benefit — it magnifies losses just as much as gains and can wipe out the entire investment quickly.", "Forgetting time decay — a long option can lose value even if the underlying is flat, simply because expiration approaches."]},
  "veh-alts": {"summary": "Alternative investments on the Series 65 are non-traditional vehicles that carry structural features candidates must recognize: leverage, derivatives exposure, credit (issuer) risk, illiquidity, and complex tax treatment. Direct participation programs organized as limited partnerships (LPs) pass income, gains, losses, and deductions directly to investors (flow-through / no entity-level tax) and split responsibility between a general partner (unlimited liability, active management) and limited partners (liability capped at their investment, plus any recourse debt assumed; passive role). Investors evaluate LPs on economic merit, not tax write-offs, and passive losses are generally deductible only against passive income.\n\nExchange-traded notes (ETNs) are unsecured, unsubordinated (senior) debt obligations of an issuing bank whose return is linked to an index; they carry the issuer's credit risk and have no principal protection, even though they trade intraday like a stock and hold no underlying basket of securities. Leveraged and inverse funds (usually ETFs) use derivatives to deliver a daily multiple (e.g., 2x, 3x) or the daily inverse (-1x, -2x) of an index's return. Because these funds reset (rebalance) daily, compounding causes their returns over periods longer than one day to diverge — often substantially — from the stated multiple of the index's cumulative return, especially in volatile or sideways markets. They are trading tools, not buy-and-hold vehicles.\n\nStructured products are securities packaged by a financial institution, typically combining a debt instrument (a zero-coupon or note component) with an embedded derivative (often an option) linked to an underlying asset, index, or basket. They too are subject to the issuer's credit risk, are generally illiquid with thin or no secondary markets, may have complex or capped payoffs, and often carry high embedded fees. Across all of these vehicles, the recurring exam themes are issuer credit risk (ETNs and structured products), daily-reset compounding/tracking error (leveraged and inverse funds), and the flow-through/liability structure of limited partnerships.", "keyPoints": ["In a limited partnership, the general partner has unlimited personal liability and manages the venture; limited partners are passive and can lose no more than their invested capital (plus any recourse debt they agreed to).", "Limited partnerships are flow-through (pass-through) entities: there is no tax at the partnership level; income, gains, losses, and deductions pass through to partners' individual returns.", "Passive activity losses from a limited partnership are generally deductible only against passive income, not against ordinary earned income or portfolio income.", "An LP investment should be judged on its economic viability, not on the tax deductions it generates; 'buying a deal for the write-offs' is a classic red flag.", "An ETN is unsecured senior debt of the issuing bank; its value depends on both index performance AND the issuer's creditworthiness, and it can go to zero if the issuer defaults.", "ETNs hold no underlying portfolio of securities (unlike ETFs), have a stated maturity, and typically avoid the tracking error an ETF can have because the issuer contractually promises the index-linked return (minus fees).", "Leveraged funds seek a daily multiple (e.g., 2x or 3x) of an index's return; inverse funds seek the daily opposite (e.g., -1x, -2x).", "Leveraged and inverse funds reset daily; over multiple days, compounding causes returns to deviate from the simple multiple of the index's cumulative move, and volatile/choppy markets erode value (volatility decay).", "Leveraged and inverse funds are designed as short-term (often intraday) tools and are generally unsuitable for long-term, buy-and-hold investors.", "Structured products combine a fixed-income (often zero-coupon) component with an embedded derivative/option tied to an underlying asset or index.", "Structured products carry the credit risk of the issuing institution; 'principal-protected' notes are only as safe as the issuer's ability to pay.", "Structured products are typically illiquid (limited or no secondary market), can have capped upside, complex payoff formulas, and high embedded costs.", "ETNs and structured products both expose the investor to issuer/credit risk; ETFs (including leveraged/inverse ETFs) hold assets and do not carry issuer credit risk in the same way."], "formulas": ["Leveraged daily return: fund daily return ≈ (multiple) × (index daily return); e.g., a 3x fund returns +3% when the index rises 1% that day.", "Inverse daily return: fund daily return ≈ (−multiple) × (index daily return); e.g., a −2x fund returns −2% when the index rises 1% that day.", "Two-day compounding example (2x fund, index +10% then −10%): Day1 = 1.00 × (1 + 0.20) = 1.20; Day2 = 1.20 × (1 − 0.20) = 0.96, a −4% cumulative result even though the index net move is −1%."], "mnemonics": ["ETN = 'Essentially a Tracking Note' backed by the bank — no assets inside, just the issuer's promise (credit risk).", "LP liability: 'Limited partner = limited loss; General partner = full guilt (unlimited liability).'", "Leveraged/Inverse funds: 'Daily reset = don't hold' — the multiple is a one-day promise, not a long-term one."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming a 2x leveraged fund held for a year returns 2x the index's annual return — daily rebalancing and compounding make longer-horizon results diverge, usually unfavorably in choppy markets.", "Confusing ETNs with ETFs: ETNs are unsecured debt with issuer credit risk and hold no securities; ETFs hold an actual portfolio.", "Believing 'principal-protected' structured products are risk-free — protection depends entirely on the issuer's solvency (credit risk), and they are often illiquid.", "Thinking limited partners can freely deduct partnership losses against salary — passive losses generally offset only passive income.", "Forgetting the general partner's unlimited liability and assuming all partners have limited liability.", "Treating leveraged/inverse funds as suitable buy-and-hold core holdings; they are short-term tactical instruments."]},
  "veh-insurance": {"summary": "Insurance-based products blend insurance features with investment or savings elements, and the Series 65 tests both their mechanics and their regulatory treatment. The central dividing line is whether the product's value fluctuates with a separate account of securities. Fixed annuities and traditional whole/term/universal life are insurance products backed by the insurer's general account; they carry insurance risk (the guarantees rest on the insurer's claims-paying ability) and are regulated by state insurance departments, not the SEC. Variable annuities and variable life insurance place premiums in a separate account of subaccounts (essentially mutual-fund-like subaccounts), shifting investment risk to the owner. Because their value depends on securities performance, they are defined as securities: they require a prospectus, must be sold with a securities license (plus a state insurance license), and are subject to FINRA suitability rules.\n\nAnnuities are accumulation-and-payout vehicles offering tax-deferred growth. Contributions to a non-qualified annuity are made with after-tax dollars, so only the earnings are taxed on withdrawal, taxed as ordinary income (not capital gains), and withdrawals before 59 1/2 generally incur a 10% penalty. Annuities use LIFO tax treatment on withdrawals, meaning taxable earnings are deemed withdrawn first. Fixed annuities pay a guaranteed minimum rate; equity indexed annuities credit interest tied to an index (subject to participation rates, caps, and spreads) with a guaranteed floor, and remain insurance products, not securities under federal law; variable annuities offer market participation with no guaranteed return and are securities.\n\nLife insurance provides a death benefit. Term is pure insurance with no cash value, lowest initial cost, and coverage for a stated period. Whole life offers a level premium, a guaranteed death benefit, and a guaranteed, tax-deferred cash value. Universal life adds premium/death-benefit flexibility with a cash value tied to current interest rates. Variable life invests cash value in separate-account subaccounts, so the death benefit and cash value fluctuate (usually with a guaranteed minimum death benefit) and it is a security. Across all life policies, the death benefit is generally income-tax-free to beneficiaries, and cash value grows tax-deferred.", "keyPoints": ["Variable annuities and variable life insurance are SECURITIES (separate account, investment risk on the owner) and require both a securities registration and an insurance license, plus prospectus delivery.", "Fixed annuities, equity indexed annuities, and traditional whole/term/universal life are INSURANCE products (general account) regulated by state insurance departments, not the SEC.", "Non-qualified annuity earnings are taxed as ordinary income (never capital gains) on withdrawal; contributions of after-tax money are returned tax-free.", "Annuity withdrawals use LIFO: taxable earnings come out first; withdrawals before age 59 1/2 face a 10% federal penalty on the taxable portion.", "Equity indexed annuities credit index-linked interest limited by participation rate, cap, and/or spread, with a guaranteed minimum floor (often around 1-3%); they are NOT securities.", "In a variable annuity the insurer bears mortality/expense risk; the contract owner bears investment risk. There is no guaranteed return in the separate account.", "Accumulation units measure the owner's interest during the pay-in phase; annuity units are fixed in number at annuitization and their value varies with separate-account performance.", "Term life = pure protection, no cash value, lowest initial premium, level or increasing premium, coverage for a set term.", "Whole life = level premium, guaranteed death benefit, and guaranteed cash value that grows tax-deferred.", "Universal life = flexible premiums and adjustable death benefit; cash value credited at current interest rates (Option A level / Option B increasing death benefit).", "Variable life = fixed scheduled premiums but cash value and death benefit vary with subaccount performance; typically includes a guaranteed minimum death benefit.", "Life insurance death benefits are generally received income-tax-free by beneficiaries; policy cash value accumulates tax-deferred.", "A 1035 exchange lets an owner swap one annuity or life policy for another (or life-to-annuity) without current taxation of gains.", "Variable contracts carry an AIR (assumed interest rate) benchmark: if actual separate-account return exceeds the AIR, the payout rises; if it lags the AIR, the payout falls."], "formulas": ["Index credit (indexed annuity) ≈ Index return × Participation rate, then limited by the Cap, minus any Spread/margin, floored at the guaranteed minimum.", "Taxable amount of non-qualified annuity withdrawal (LIFO) = withdrawal amount up to accumulated earnings taxed as ordinary income; remainder (return of principal) is tax-free.", "Number of annuity units = fixed at annuitization; monthly payout = annuity units × current annuity unit value (which moves with separate-account performance vs. AIR)."], "mnemonics": ["'Variable = Vulnerable to the market = a Security' (separate account; owner bears investment risk).", "Annuity taxation LIFO: 'Last In, First Out' — gains (last in) come out first and are taxed.", "Universal life death benefit: 'A = level (like whole life), B = Bigger/increasing.'", "Life insurance types: 'Term Wants Universal Variety' = Term, Whole, Universal, Variable."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming annuity gains get favorable capital-gains treatment — they are always ordinary income; the annuity converts capital gains into ordinary income.", "Thinking an equity indexed annuity is a security — it is an insurance product (general account, guaranteed floor); only VARIABLE annuities are securities.", "Confusing who bears risk in a variable annuity — the OWNER bears investment risk; the insurer bears mortality and expense risk.", "Believing term life builds cash value — it does not; only whole, universal, and variable life have cash value.", "Assuming variable life has no death-benefit guarantee — most have a guaranteed MINIMUM death benefit even though cash value can fall to zero from poor performance.", "Forgetting the 10% pre-59 1/2 penalty applies to the taxable (earnings) portion of annuity withdrawals, layered on top of ordinary income tax.", "Confusing accumulation units (pay-in phase, number varies) with annuity units (payout phase, number fixed, value varies)."]},
  "veh-other": {"summary": "The \"Other Assets\" category on the Series 65 covers investment vehicles outside traditional stocks, bonds, and pooled funds - principally commodities/precious metals and, increasingly, digital assets. Commodities are physical goods (agricultural products, energy, industrial and precious metals) that trade in spot and, more commonly for investors, futures markets. Because most commodity exposure is obtained through futures contracts, options, or commodity pools rather than direct ownership of the physical good, candidates must understand who regulates these instruments and how the exposure is packaged. The CFTC (Commodity Futures Trading Commission) and the NFA (National Futures Association), not the SEC, regulate commodity futures and most commodity trading advisers; standard futures contracts are generally not \"securities\" under the Uniform Securities Act.\n\nPrecious metals - gold, silver, platinum, palladium - are viewed on the exam as inflation hedges and 'stores of value' with low or negative correlation to financial assets, but they produce no income (no dividends or interest), carry storage/insurance costs, and can be highly volatile. Physical bullion held directly is not a security; however, ETFs and mutual funds that hold or track metals ARE securities and are regulated accordingly. A key exam theme is that commodities/metals add diversification because of low correlation, but they are non-income-producing and speculative.\n\nDigital assets (cryptocurrencies and tokens) are the newest 'other asset.' The exam framing: many crypto tokens are treated as securities when they satisfy the Howey investment-contract test (an investment of money in a common enterprise with an expectation of profits derived from the efforts of others). Bitcoin is generally viewed by the SEC/CFTC as a commodity rather than a security. Digital assets are extremely volatile, largely non-income-producing, and carry unique risks: no FDIC/SIPC coverage, custody/private-key loss, hacking, fraud, illiquidity, and evolving regulation. NASAA has repeatedly flagged crypto and crypto-related offerings as leading sources of investor complaints and fraud, so suitability, disclosure, and fiduciary care are heavily tested.", "keyPoints": ["Commodity futures are regulated by the CFTC and NFA, not the SEC; standard futures contracts are generally NOT 'securities' under the Uniform Securities Act.", "Precious metals and commodities are non-income-producing assets - they pay no dividends or interest and incur storage/insurance costs.", "The main portfolio rationale for commodities/precious metals is diversification: low or negative correlation with stocks and bonds, plus inflation hedging.", "Physical bullion held directly is not a security, but ETFs, mutual funds, and commodity pools that provide metal/commodity exposure ARE securities.", "Gold and other metals are volatile and speculative; suitability requires adequate risk tolerance and typically only a modest portfolio allocation.", "Under the Howey test, an instrument is an 'investment contract' (a security) if there is (1) an investment of money, (2) in a common enterprise, (3) with an expectation of profits, (4) derived predominantly from the efforts of others.", "Many crypto tokens - especially those sold in ICOs to fund a venture - are treated as securities; Bitcoin is generally treated as a commodity (CFTC jurisdiction) rather than a security.", "Digital assets carry NO FDIC or SIPC protection and are exposed to custody/private-key loss, exchange hacks, extreme volatility, illiquidity, and fraud.", "NASAA consistently ranks crypto-related products among the top investor threats; heightened disclosure and suitability scrutiny apply to any crypto recommendation.", "Commodity pools that are structured as investment companies or that offer securities can fall under securities regulation even though the underlying is a commodity.", "Investment advisers recommending commodities or digital assets remain fiduciaries: they must have a reasonable basis, disclose material risks and conflicts, and confirm suitability regardless of whether the asset itself is a 'security'.", "Precious-metals and crypto exposure can be gained indirectly through ETFs, ETNs, futures, or funds - each wrapper changes liquidity, tax treatment, tracking error, and counterparty/credit risk."], "mnemonics": ["Howey = 'I-C-E-O': Investment of money, Common enterprise, Expectation of profit, Others' efforts - satisfy all four and it's a security.", "'Metals: no yield, just shield' - precious metals produce no income; they serve as an inflation/crisis hedge and diversifier."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming ALL cryptocurrency is a security - Bitcoin is generally a commodity; the security determination depends on the Howey test applied to the specific token/offering.", "Thinking commodity futures fall under the SEC and the Uniform Securities Act - they are primarily CFTC/NFA-regulated and generally not 'securities'.", "Believing precious metals pay income - they pay none and add storage/insurance costs; their value comes from price appreciation and diversification only.", "Assuming a crypto account or crypto exchange offers FDIC or SIPC protection - it does not.", "Confusing 'not a security' with 'no fiduciary duty' - an adviser recommending non-security commodities or crypto still owes suitability, disclosure, and reasonable-basis obligations.", "Over-allocating to commodities/crypto for a conservative or income-focused client - both are volatile, speculative, and non-income-producing."]},
  "rec-client-type": {"summary": "The \"type of client\" determines who has legal authority to open an account, who can bind the account to transactions, whose objectives and risk tolerance govern the recommendation, and how the account is taxed. For a natural person the individual acts for themselves; a sole proprietorship is not a separate legal entity, so the owner and the business are the same taxpayer and the same account holder. Business entities (partnerships, LLCs, corporations) are separate legal persons that must open accounts in the entity name, and the adviser must obtain the governing document (partnership agreement, operating agreement, or corporate resolution) identifying who is authorized to trade.\n\nTrusts and estates are legal arrangements managed by a fiduciary (trustee or executor/administrator) who must invest under the terms of the governing instrument and applicable law, most notably the Uniform Prudent Investor Act (UPIA). Under UPIA, prudence is judged at the portfolio level in the context of an overall strategy, diversification is presumptively required, and the fiduciary balances the interests of income beneficiaries and remaindermen. The trust document and state law -- not the trustee's personal goals -- control the objectives.\n\nFoundations and charitable/endowment funds are typically tax-exempt and long-horizon, focused on preserving purchasing power (real, inflation-adjusted capital) while funding grants or spending each year. Charitable and endowment funds are frequently governed by the Uniform Prudent Management of Institutional Funds Act (UPMIFA). For every entity or fiduciary account, the adviser evaluates suitability against the entity's/beneficiaries' objectives and its governing documents, not the individual signer's personal situation.", "keyPoints": ["A sole proprietorship is NOT a separate legal entity; the owner and business are one taxpayer, and income is reported on the owner's individual return (Schedule C).", "A natural person is an individual human being; a 'legal person' includes entities like corporations and LLCs that can own property and contract.", "To open an entity account, the adviser must obtain the governing document and evidence of authority: partnership agreement (partnership), operating agreement (LLC), or corporate resolution (corporation).", "General partners have unlimited personal liability and management authority; limited partners have limited liability but generally cannot manage without losing that protection.", "C corporations are taxed at the entity level and dividends again at the shareholder level (double taxation); S corporations are pass-through entities (income flows to shareholders).", "LLC income is typically pass-through by default; the operating agreement names the members/managers authorized to act for the account.", "A trust is managed by a trustee who is a fiduciary bound by the trust instrument and, in most states, the Uniform Prudent Investor Act (UPIA).", "Under UPIA prudence is measured at the total-portfolio level, diversification is presumptively required, and the trustee must balance income beneficiaries against remaindermen.", "An estate is administered by an executor (named in a will) or an administrator (appointed when there is no will); investing is typically conservative pending distribution.", "Foundations, endowments, and charities are usually tax-exempt with long time horizons; a common objective is preserving inflation-adjusted (real) purchasing power while funding annual spending.", "Charitable/institutional funds are commonly governed by UPMIFA (Uniform Prudent Management of Institutional Funds Act), the institutional analog to UPIA.", "For any fiduciary or entity account, suitability is judged against the entity's or beneficiaries' objectives and governing documents -- never the personal goals of the individual signer."], "formulas": ["Real (inflation-adjusted) return ≈ Nominal return − Inflation rate (used to test whether a foundation/endowment preserves purchasing power)", "Endowment/foundation total-return need ≈ Annual spending rate (%) + Inflation rate (%) as a rough required real+inflation gross return target"], "mnemonics": ["POLICE the authority: Partnership agreement, Operating agreement, corporate resoLution, Investment authority, Certification, Estate letters -- always verify WHO can trade before acting.", "'TRUST the document, not the trustee' -- the instrument and UPIA control objectives, not the fiduciary's personal preferences.", "PIE for entity taxation: Pass-through (S corp, most LLCs, partnerships) vs. Independent/Entity-level tax (C corp) -- and sole proprietorship = owner's own return."], "pitfalls": ["Treating a sole proprietorship as a separate taxpayer/entity -- it is not; the owner reports business income on their personal return.", "Assuming the trustee's or corporate officer's personal risk tolerance sets the account objective -- suitability follows the trust/entity and its beneficiaries, per the governing document.", "Confusing executor (named in a will) with administrator (court-appointed when there is no will).", "Forgetting that UPIA judges prudence at the whole-portfolio level and presumes diversification -- a single 'risky' holding is not automatically imprudent.", "Assuming limited partners can actively manage the business -- doing so can cost them their limited-liability protection.", "Recommending heavily tax-favored products (e.g., municipal bonds) to a tax-exempt foundation or charity -- the tax exemption is wasted since the entity already pays no tax.", "Mixing up C-corp double taxation with S-corp/LLC pass-through treatment."]},
  "rec-profile": {"summary": "Before any recommendation can meet the suitability and fiduciary standard, an investment adviser must build a complete client profile. This profile has both financial and nonfinancial dimensions. The financial side covers the client's goals and objectives (e.g., retirement, education funding, capital preservation, growth, income), their current and projected financial situation (income and cash flow, a balance sheet of assets and liabilities, existing investments, tax bracket, and expected resources such as Social Security and pensions), and their time horizon for each goal. The nonfinancial side captures risk tolerance, values and attitudes, investment experience, demographics, life events, and behavioral tendencies.\n\nData gathering is the foundation. Advisers collect information through client identification (including AML/KYC and CIP requirements), written questionnaires, and personal interviews, and they must periodically update the profile because circumstances change. A key distinction on the exam is between risk tolerance (a client's willingness and emotional ability to endure volatility and loss) and risk capacity (the objective, financial ability to absorb loss based on time horizon, income, net worth, and liquidity). A young investor may have high risk capacity but low tolerance, or vice versa; the adviser must reconcile the two.\n\nTime horizon is central because it drives asset allocation: longer horizons permit greater equity exposure and can absorb short-term volatility, while short horizons demand liquidity and capital preservation. Behavioral finance concepts (loss aversion, overconfidence, anchoring, herding, recency bias, mental accounting, framing, confirmation bias) explain why clients deviate from rational decisions, and a good adviser recognizes and manages these biases rather than indulging them. Ultimately, all profile elements must be integrated: goals plus time horizon plus risk tolerance and capacity plus tax and liquidity constraints together determine an appropriate, suitable strategy.", "keyPoints": ["A complete client profile includes both financial factors (goals, cash flow, balance sheet, existing holdings, taxes, time horizon) and nonfinancial factors (risk tolerance, values, experience, life events, behavioral biases).", "Risk tolerance = willingness/emotional ability to accept volatility; risk capacity = objective financial ability to absorb loss. They are distinct and can conflict.", "Longer time horizon generally allows greater equity/growth exposure; short time horizon requires liquidity and capital preservation regardless of stated risk tolerance.", "Nonfinancial considerations include age, family/dependents, marital status, health, employment, investment experience, values (e.g., ESG or religious screens), and life events (birth, divorce, inheritance, job loss).", "Data-gathering methods: client identification (CIP/KYC/AML), written questionnaires, and personal interviews; the profile must be updated when circumstances materially change.", "A balance sheet lists assets minus liabilities to show net worth (a point-in-time snapshot); a cash flow statement shows income minus expenses over a period (measures surplus available to invest).", "Non-discretionary resources like Social Security and defined-benefit pensions act like bond-like income streams and reduce the need for fixed-income allocation elsewhere.", "Tax situation (marginal bracket, account type) affects recommendations: high-bracket clients may favor municipal bonds and tax-deferred vehicles; asset location matters.", "Investment objectives commonly include capital preservation, income, growth, growth and income, speculation, and liquidity - often in tension (e.g., high income vs. high growth).", "Emergency fund/liquidity needs and existing insurance coverage should be assessed before committing funds to long-term illiquid investments.", "Behavioral biases: loss aversion, overconfidence, anchoring, herding, recency bias, confirmation bias, mental accounting, and framing cause clients to act irrationally.", "The profile drives suitability: a recommendation must fit the client's goals, horizon, risk profile, tax status, and liquidity needs - not the adviser's preferences.", "Time horizon is goal-specific: one client can have multiple goals (near-term home purchase, long-term retirement) each with its own horizon and allocation."], "formulas": ["Net Worth = Total Assets - Total Liabilities (balance sheet)", "Net (Discretionary) Cash Flow = Total Income - Total Expenses (cash flow statement)", "After-tax yield = Taxable yield x (1 - marginal tax rate)", "Taxable-equivalent yield = Municipal (tax-free) yield / (1 - marginal tax rate)"], "mnemonics": ["Objectives ('PIGSL'): Preservation (safety), Income, Growth, Speculation, and Liquidity - with 'growth and income' being a blend of the middle objectives.", "Tolerance vs. Capacity: 'Tolerance is in the heart, Capacity is on the balance sheet.'"], "pitfalls": ["Confusing risk tolerance (emotional willingness) with risk capacity (financial ability) - the exam tests the difference.", "Assuming a long time horizon automatically means high risk tolerance; horizon affects capacity, not necessarily willingness.", "Treating a balance sheet and a cash flow statement as interchangeable - one is a point-in-time snapshot of net worth, the other measures income vs. expenses over a period.", "Recommending an illiquid or aggressive product before confirming an adequate emergency fund and the client's actual liquidity needs.", "Forgetting that guaranteed income (pension, Social Security) reduces the need for additional fixed income and can allow a higher equity allocation.", "Indulging a client's behavioral bias (e.g., chasing recent winners) instead of identifying and counseling against it."]},
  "rec-cmt": {"summary": "Capital Market Theory on the Series 65 centers on three interrelated frameworks that explain how rational investors should build portfolios and how prices behave in efficient markets: Modern Portfolio Theory (MPT), the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM), and the Efficient Market Hypothesis (EMH). MPT, developed by Harry Markowitz, holds that investors are risk-averse and evaluate portfolios by their expected return and total risk (standard deviation), seeking the highest return for a given level of risk. Its central insight is diversification: combining assets whose returns are not perfectly positively correlated reduces portfolio risk without proportionally reducing return. Portfolios offering the best risk/return trade-off form the efficient frontier, and rational investors select a portfolio on that frontier matching their risk tolerance.\n\nCAPM builds on MPT by separating total risk into systematic (market) risk and unsystematic (diversifiable, company-specific) risk. Diversification eliminates unsystematic risk, so the market only rewards investors for bearing systematic risk, measured by beta. The Security Market Line (SML) plots required return against beta, and CAPM's formula prices any security's required return as the risk-free rate plus a risk premium proportional to beta. Beta of 1.0 moves with the market; above 1.0 is more volatile; below 1.0 is less volatile.\n\nEMH asserts that security prices fully and rapidly reflect available information, so consistently 'beating the market' on a risk-adjusted basis through security selection or timing is not reliably possible. Its three forms differ by the information already impounded in price: weak (all past price/volume data, undermining technical analysis), semi-strong (all public information, undermining fundamental analysis of public data), and strong (all information including material non-public, implying even insiders cannot consistently profit). EMH is the intellectual case for passive/index investing. Behavioral finance offers the main critique, arguing investor psychology produces anomalies inconsistent with fully rational, efficient pricing.", "keyPoints": ["Modern Portfolio Theory (Markowitz) assumes investors are risk-averse and judge a portfolio by expected return and total risk (standard deviation), not by individual securities in isolation.", "Diversification works by combining assets with low or negative correlation; the lower the correlation between assets, the greater the reduction in portfolio risk.", "The efficient frontier is the set of portfolios offering the maximum expected return for each level of risk; rational investors choose a portfolio ON the frontier, never below it.", "Total risk = systematic risk + unsystematic risk. Systematic (market) risk cannot be diversified away; unsystematic (business/company-specific) risk can be.", "Beta measures systematic (market) risk. Beta 1.0 = moves with market; beta > 1.0 = more volatile than market; beta < 1.0 = less volatile; the market portfolio has beta of exactly 1.0.", "Standard deviation measures TOTAL risk (appropriate for a whole portfolio); beta measures only systematic risk (appropriate for a diversified portfolio or individual stock relative to the market).", "CAPM required return = risk-free rate + beta x (expected market return - risk-free rate); the term (market return - risk-free rate) is the market risk premium.", "The Security Market Line (SML) plots required return against beta; the Capital Market Line (CML) plots return against standard deviation (total risk).", "Under CAPM, investors are only compensated for bearing systematic risk because unsystematic risk can be eliminated through diversification.", "EMH weak form: prices reflect all past price and trading-volume data, so technical analysis cannot produce consistent excess returns.", "EMH semi-strong form: prices reflect all publicly available information, so neither technical nor fundamental analysis of public data yields consistent excess returns.", "EMH strong form: prices reflect all information, public and private, so even insiders with material non-public information cannot consistently earn excess returns.", "EMH is the theoretical justification for passive/indexed investing; behavioral finance is the main challenge, citing anomalies driven by investor psychology.", "A security plotting ABOVE the SML is undervalued (offers more return than required for its beta); one plotting BELOW the SML is overvalued."], "formulas": ["CAPM (required/expected return): r = Rf + β x (Rm - Rf), where Rf = risk-free rate, Rm = expected market return, β = beta.", "Market risk premium = Rm - Rf.", "Total risk = Systematic (non-diversifiable) risk + Unsystematic (diversifiable) risk.", "Example: Rf = 3%, Rm = 9%, β = 1.5  ->  r = 3% + 1.5 x (9% - 3%) = 3% + 9% = 12%."], "mnemonics": ["EMH forms from weakest to strongest information set: Weak = 'Watch the charts won't work' (past prices), Semi-strong = 'Public news is already in', Strong = 'Secrets too' (even insider info priced in).", "'Systematic = market-wide, can't Split it away; Unsystematic = single company, Undo it with diversification.'", "SML uses Beta (Systematic risk); CML uses standard deviation (Complete/total risk)."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing standard deviation with beta: standard deviation measures TOTAL risk; beta measures ONLY systematic (market) risk. The exam tests which is appropriate for a diversified portfolio (beta) vs. total risk (standard deviation).", "Assuming diversification eliminates ALL risk. It only removes unsystematic risk; systematic (market) risk remains no matter how diversified the portfolio.", "Mixing up the SML and the CML. The SML's x-axis is beta; the CML's x-axis is standard deviation. Both plot return on the y-axis.", "In CAPM, forgetting that beta multiplies only the risk premium (Rm - Rf), not the entire market return. A common wrong calculation multiplies beta by Rm.", "Thinking semi-strong EMH still leaves room for fundamental analysis. Under semi-strong, all PUBLIC information (including financial statements) is already priced, so fundamental analysis of public data cannot yield consistent excess returns.", "Believing strong-form EMH permits insiders to profit. Strong form specifically says even material non-public information is already reflected in price.", "A security plotting above the SML is UNDERvalued (buy), not overvalued; students often reverse this."]},
  "rec-portfolio": {"summary": "Portfolio management on the Series 65 divides into strategies (the high-level asset-allocation plan), styles (the philosophy guiding security selection), and techniques (specific tactical tools used to implement or adjust the portfolio). Strategic asset allocation sets long-term target weights among asset classes based on the client's objectives and periodically rebalances back to those targets, implicitly a buy-and-hold, mean-reversion belief. Tactical asset allocation deliberately deviates from those targets in the short run to exploit perceived market opportunities, then returns to the strategic baseline, making it an active timing overlay.\n\nInvestment styles describe how a manager picks securities. Active management seeks to outperform a benchmark through selection and timing (higher cost, higher turnover), while passive (indexing) simply replicates a benchmark at low cost, reflecting a belief in efficient markets. Growth investing targets companies with rapid earnings/revenue expansion and high P/E ratios; value investing seeks underpriced securities with low P/E, low price-to-book, and often higher dividend yields. Income investing emphasizes current cash flow (dividends, interest), whereas capital appreciation prioritizes price growth over current income.\n\nTechniques are the tools used to build and manage the portfolio: diversification to reduce unsystematic risk; sector rotation to shift among industries across the business cycle; dollar-cost averaging to invest fixed dollar amounts on a schedule; options for hedging or income; leverage/margin to amplify returns and risk; volatility management; inverse (short) strategies to profit from declines; and high-frequency trading (HFT) that uses algorithms and speed for tiny, frequent gains. Candidates must know that diversification reduces only unsystematic (nonsystematic) risk, not systematic (market) risk, and that leverage and inverse/HFT strategies magnify risk and are generally unsuitable for conservative clients.", "keyPoints": ["Strategic asset allocation = fixed long-term target weights with periodic rebalancing; a passive, buy-and-hold-oriented approach that sells winners and buys losers to restore targets.", "Tactical asset allocation = short-term, intentional deviations from strategic targets to exploit market opportunities, then reverting to baseline; it is a form of market timing (active).", "Active management aims to beat a benchmark via selection/timing; it has higher expenses, higher turnover, and greater tax drag. Passive (index) management matches a benchmark at low cost, low turnover.", "Growth style: high earnings/revenue growth, high P/E, little or no dividend, higher volatility. Value style: low P/E, low price-to-book, often higher dividend yield, seen as underpriced.", "Income style targets current cash flow (dividends/interest); capital-appreciation style targets price growth and reinvestment over current income.", "Diversification reduces unsystematic (business/industry-specific) risk only; it cannot eliminate systematic (market) risk. Adding uncorrelated assets lowers portfolio standard deviation.", "Sector rotation shifts allocations among industry sectors based on the phase of the business/economic cycle (e.g., defensive sectors late-cycle, cyclicals early-cycle).", "Dollar-cost averaging invests a fixed DOLLAR amount at regular intervals, buying more shares when prices are low and fewer when high, producing an average cost per share at or below the simple average of the prices.", "Leverage (buying on margin) magnifies both gains and losses and adds interest cost; it raises risk and is generally unsuitable for conservative, income-focused clients.", "Inverse (short) strategies profit when the underlying falls; leveraged/inverse ETFs reset daily and suffer compounding decay, making them short-term trading tools, not buy-and-hold holdings.", "Options can hedge (protective put), generate income (covered call), or speculate; they are common volatility-management tools.", "High-frequency trading (HFT) uses powerful algorithms and ultra-low latency to execute huge volumes of orders for tiny per-trade profits; it is an institutional technique, not a retail strategy."], "formulas": ["Dollar-cost averaging average cost per share = Total dollars invested / Total shares purchased (a harmonic mean of the purchase prices, always <= the simple arithmetic average of those prices).", "Rebalancing to strategic targets: if an asset class's weight drifts above target, sell the excess; if below target, buy — restoring the original percentage mix."], "mnemonics": ["Strategic = Set-and-rebalance (long-term Static targets); Tactical = Timing (short-term Tilts).", "Value = 'value shopper' looks for LOW prices (low P/E, low P/B); Growth = paying UP for fast growth (high P/E).", "DCA: fixed DOLLARS, not fixed shares — you buy MORE when it's cheap."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing dollar-cost averaging (fixed dollar amount each period) with buying a fixed number of shares each period — only the fixed-dollar version guarantees an average cost at or below the average price.", "Believing diversification reduces systematic/market risk — it only reduces unsystematic risk. Beta/market risk remains.", "Assuming leveraged and inverse ETFs are suitable long-term holdings — daily resetting causes compounding decay, so returns over long horizons diverge from a simple multiple of the index.", "Mislabeling strategic allocation as active or tactical allocation as passive — strategic is the long-term baseline (passive-leaning) and tactical is the short-term active overlay.", "Assuming active management is superior because it costs more — higher fees and turnover often cause it to underperform its index net of costs.", "Treating high dividend yield as a growth trait — high current yield and low P/E are hallmarks of value/income, not growth."]},
  "rec-tax": {"summary": "Tax considerations shape nearly every advisory recommendation. For individuals, the key distinctions are between ordinary income (wages, interest, non-qualified dividends, short-term gains) taxed at graduated marginal rates, and preferentially taxed items: long-term capital gains (asset held MORE than one year) and qualified dividends, both taxed at 0%/15%/20% depending on income. Cost basis determines gain or loss on sale; it is adjusted for reinvested dividends, commissions, and return of capital, and anchors tax-loss harvesting (subject to the 30-day wash-sale rule). The Alternative Minimum Tax (AMT) is a parallel calculation that adds back certain preference items (e.g., interest on private-activity municipal bonds, the bargain element on incentive stock options) and applies an exemption; the taxpayer pays the HIGHER of regular tax or AMT.\n\nEntities are taxed differently. C corporations pay a corporate-level income tax and shareholders are taxed again on dividends (double taxation); corporations receive a dividends-received deduction on dividends from other domestic corporations. Pass-through entities (S corporations, partnerships, LLCs) generally pay no entity-level tax; income flows through to owners on a Schedule K-1. Trusts and estates are separate taxpayers that file Form 1041 and use highly COMPRESSED brackets, reaching the top marginal rate at a very low income level, which pushes fiduciaries to distribute income to beneficiaries (income taxed to whoever receives it; distributable net income, DNI, caps the amount taxed to beneficiaries and deductible by the trust).\n\nWealth transfer is governed by the UNIFIED gift and estate tax system, sharing one lifetime exemption (credit) and a top 40% rate. The annual gift tax exclusion lets a donor give a set amount per recipient per year with no gift-tax return and no use of the lifetime exemption; unlimited marital and charitable deductions defer or eliminate transfer tax. A pivotal planning distinction: property transferred at DEATH receives a stepped-up basis to fair market value, while a lifetime GIFT carries over the donor's basis (carryover basis). This drives the decision to gift low-basis assets during life versus holding them to receive the step-up.", "keyPoints": ["Long-term capital gains require holding an asset MORE than one year (a one-year-and-a-day hold); one year or less is short-term and taxed as ordinary income.", "Long-term capital gains and qualified dividends share the preferential 0%/15%/20% federal rate schedule; non-qualified dividends and interest are ordinary income.", "For a stock dividend to be 'qualified,' the shares generally must be held more than 60 days during the 121-day period around the ex-dividend date and paid by a U.S. or qualified foreign corporation.", "Cost basis is increased by reinvested dividends and purchase commissions and reduced by return-of-capital distributions; it determines taxable gain or loss.", "The wash-sale rule disallows a loss if a substantially identical security is bought within 30 days before or after the sale; the disallowed loss is added to the new position's basis.", "Net capital losses can offset capital gains plus up to $3,000 of ordinary income per year, with the excess carried forward indefinitely.", "AMT is a parallel tax: add back preference items (private-activity muni bond interest, ISO bargain element, certain deductions), subtract the exemption, and pay the greater of AMT or regular tax.", "C corporations face double taxation (entity tax + shareholder tax on dividends); the dividends-received deduction reduces tax on dividends a corporation receives from other domestic corporations.", "S corporations, partnerships, and most LLCs are pass-through entities: no entity-level federal income tax; income and character flow to owners via Schedule K-1.", "Trusts and estates file Form 1041 and hit the top marginal bracket at a very low income threshold (compressed brackets), incentivizing distributions to beneficiaries.", "Distributable net income (DNI) limits the income taxed to beneficiaries and the trust's distribution deduction; income keeps its character (e.g., tax-exempt) as it passes through.", "The estate and gift taxes are unified, sharing one lifetime exemption and a top rate of 40%.", "The annual gift tax exclusion applies per donor, per recipient, per year, requires no gift-tax filing, and does NOT reduce the lifetime exemption.", "Assets included in a decedent's estate receive a stepped-up (or stepped-down) basis to date-of-death fair market value; lifetime gifts take a carryover basis from the donor.", "Transfers to a U.S.-citizen spouse and to qualified charities qualify for unlimited marital and charitable deductions, avoiding transfer tax."], "formulas": ["Capital gain/loss = Net sale proceeds - Adjusted cost basis", "Adjusted basis = Purchase price + commissions + reinvested dividends - return of capital", "Net investment capital loss deductible against ordinary income = up to $3,000/year (excess carried forward)", "AMT (simplified) = (Regular taxable income + preference/adjustment add-backs - AMT exemption) x AMT rate; taxpayer pays greater of this or regular tax", "After-tax return = Pre-tax return x (1 - marginal or capital-gains tax rate)", "Taxable-equivalent yield = Tax-free (municipal) yield / (1 - marginal tax rate)"], "mnemonics": ["'Held a year AND a day' = long-term treatment (must exceed one year).", "'Gift = Give your basis; Death = Deduct nothing, step it Up' — gifts carry over basis, inherited property steps up to FMV.", "'DNI = Don't Nail It twice' — DNI ensures income is taxed once, to either the trust or the beneficiary, not both.", "AMT add-backs 'PIP': Private-activity muni interest, Incentive stock options, Preference deductions."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming ALL dividends are taxed at the 15% rate: only QUALIFIED dividends get the preferential rate; non-qualified (e.g., most REIT and money-market dividends) are ordinary income.", "Treating a security held exactly one year as long-term: the hold must EXCEED one year (one year plus a day).", "Believing a lifetime gift gives the recipient a stepped-up basis: gifts carry over the donor's basis; only property transferred at death gets the step-up.", "Confusing the annual gift exclusion with the lifetime exemption: gifts under the annual exclusion never touch the lifetime exemption and require no filing.", "Forgetting the wash-sale rule when harvesting losses: repurchasing a substantially identical security within 30 days disallows the loss.", "Assuming trusts enjoy the same wide brackets as individuals: trust brackets are compressed and reach the top rate at a very low income level.", "Thinking municipal bond interest is always tax-free for AMT: private-activity municipal bond interest is an AMT preference item that must be added back."]},
  "rec-retirement": {"summary": "Retirement plans divide into two broad legal buckets. QUALIFIED plans meet IRS Code Section 401(a) requirements and receive favorable tax treatment: contributions are generally pre-tax (deductible), earnings grow tax-deferred, and distributions are taxed as ordinary income when taken. Qualified plans (401(k), profit-sharing, defined benefit/pension, Keogh) must be nondiscriminatory, must not favor highly compensated employees, and are protected by ERISA. NONQUALIFIED plans (deferred compensation, payroll deduction, Section 457 for governmental/tax-exempt employers) do NOT meet 401(a), can discriminate (e.g., reward only key executives), but offer no employer tax deduction until the employee actually receives the money, and the funds generally remain subject to the employer's creditors.\n\nIRAs are individually established. A TRADITIONAL IRA allows potentially deductible contributions (deductibility phases out if the owner or spouse is an active participant in an employer plan and income is high), tax-deferred growth, and fully/partially taxable distributions. A ROTH IRA takes after-tax (nondeductible) contributions but grows and distributes tax-free if qualified (account open 5 years AND age 59 1/2, death, disability, or first-home). Roth contributions phase out at higher MAGI; traditional contributions have no income cap (only deductibility does). Both share one combined annual contribution limit.\n\nA SOLO 401(k) (individual/one-participant 401(k)) covers a self-employed person with no employees other than a spouse. The owner contributes in two capacities: as employEE (elective deferral) and as employER (profit-sharing), allowing much larger totals than an IRA. It can offer both traditional (pre-tax) and Roth (after-tax) elective deferrals. Key exam themes: RMDs, the 10% early-withdrawal penalty before 59 1/2, ordinary-income taxation of pre-tax distributions, ERISA coverage, and the qualified vs. nonqualified distinction.", "keyPoints": ["Qualified plans (401(a)/ERISA) offer an employer tax deduction, must be nondiscriminatory, and cannot favor highly compensated employees; nonqualified plans may discriminate and favor key executives.", "In a nonqualified deferred compensation plan the employer gets NO deduction until the employee is actually paid, and deferred amounts generally remain reachable by the employer's creditors (unfunded promise).", "Traditional IRA contributions may be tax-deductible; deductibility phases out for active participants in an employer plan at higher income, but anyone with earned income can still contribute nondeductibly.", "Roth IRA contributions are never deductible; qualified distributions are tax-free if the 5-year holding period is met AND the owner is 59 1/2, deceased, disabled, or a first-time homebuyer.", "Roth IRA eligibility to contribute phases out at higher MAGI; traditional IRAs have no income limit on contributing (only on deducting).", "Distributions from traditional IRAs and pre-tax qualified plans are taxed as ordinary income, never as capital gains, regardless of what the account earned.", "The 10% early-withdrawal penalty generally applies to distributions before age 59 1/2 (with exceptions such as death, disability, first-home up to $10,000 for IRAs, qualified education for IRAs, medical, and substantially equal periodic payments).", "Roth IRAs have no required minimum distributions during the owner's lifetime; traditional IRAs and pre-tax qualified plans require RMDs starting at the current statutory age.", "A Solo 401(k) lets a self-employed owner contribute both as employee (elective deferral) and as employer (profit-sharing), producing far higher limits than an IRA.", "A Solo 401(k) is only available to a business with no full-time common-law employees other than the owner and the owner's spouse; hiring eligible employees ends eligibility.", "Roth Solo 401(k) deferrals are after-tax; the employer profit-sharing portion is traditionally pre-tax and grows tax-deferred.", "Excess IRA contributions (over the annual limit or exceeding earned income) trigger a 6% excise tax each year until corrected.", "Keogh (HR-10) plans are qualified plans for self-employed individuals/unincorporated businesses; ERISA generally protects qualified-plan assets from creditors.", "Section 457 plans are nonqualified deferred compensation plans for governmental and certain tax-exempt employers."], "formulas": ["Qualified/traditional-IRA taxable distribution = full distribution taxed as ordinary income (basis = 0 if all contributions were deductible/pre-tax).", "Roth qualified distribution tax = $0 (tax-free) when 5-year rule met AND a qualifying event (59 1/2, death, disability, first home) occurs.", "Early-withdrawal cost (pre-59 1/2, no exception) = ordinary income tax on taxable amount + 10% penalty on taxable amount.", "Solo 401(k) total = employee elective deferral + employer profit-sharing contribution (subject to annual dollar/percentage caps)."], "mnemonics": ["QUALIFIED = 'Qualifies for a deduction' (employer deducts now, employee taxed later); NONqualified = 'Not deductible now' (employer deducts only when employee is paid).", "ROTH = 'Return Of Taxed money Hopefully tax-free' - pay tax on the seed, harvest tax-free.", "Solo 401(k) 'wears two hats': employEE deferral + employER profit-share.", "5 AND (59 1/2 / death / disability / first home) = the Roth qualified-distribution test."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming traditional IRA/qualified-plan withdrawals get capital-gains treatment - they are ALWAYS ordinary income.", "Confusing the Roth CONTRIBUTION income phase-out with a (nonexistent) traditional IRA contribution income limit; traditional IRAs cap only DEDUCTIBILITY, not the ability to contribute.", "Thinking a nonqualified deferred-comp plan gives the employer an immediate deduction - the deduction is deferred until the employee actually receives payment.", "Believing Roth IRAs require lifetime RMDs - they do not for the original owner (though inherited Roths have their own rules).", "Forgetting a Solo 401(k) is disqualified once the business hires a non-spouse full-time employee.", "Assuming the 5-year clock plus age 59 1/2 alone is enough for tax-free Roth earnings - you need BOTH the 5-year period AND a qualifying event.", "Treating nonqualified plans as ERISA-protected from creditors - the deferred funds generally remain the employer's assets and are exposed to the employer's creditors."]},
  "rec-erisa": {"summary": "The Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974 (ERISA) governs most private-sector retirement plans. Anyone with discretionary authority over plan assets or administration - or who renders investment advice for a fee - is an ERISA fiduciary and is held to the 'prudent expert' standard: acting solely in the interest of participants and beneficiaries, for the exclusive purpose of providing benefits and defraying reasonable expenses, with the care, skill, prudence, and diligence of a knowledgeable professional familiar with such matters, and diversifying investments to minimize the risk of large losses. Investment advisers who advise plans should understand these duties because they may themselves become co-fiduciaries.\n\nERISA Section 404(c) offers fiduciaries relief from liability for investment losses in participant-directed plans (such as 401(k)s). If the plan gives participants genuine control and offers a 'broad range' of investment alternatives - generally at least three diversified options with materially different risk-and-return characteristics, allowing participants to build a diversified portfolio and to change allocations with a frequency appropriate to volatility (at least quarterly) - then the participant, not the fiduciary, bears responsibility for the results of their own investment choices. The fiduciary is never relieved of the duty to prudently select and monitor the menu of options offered.\n\nAn Investment Policy Statement (IPS) is a written document that establishes the plan's investment objectives, risk tolerance, permitted asset classes, and criteria for selecting, monitoring, and replacing investments. ERISA does not statutorily require an IPS, but the Department of Labor and courts treat maintaining and following one as evidence of a prudent process; it is considered a fiduciary best practice. The IPS guides decisions but is a set of guidelines, not a rigid contract, and it should be reviewed periodically.\n\nERISA Section 406 forbids 'prohibited transactions' between a plan and a 'party in interest' (which the Internal Revenue Code calls a 'disqualified person') - including sales, exchanges, or leases of property; lending money or extending credit; furnishing goods, services, or facilities; and transferring plan assets to such a party. Section 406(b) separately bars fiduciary self-dealing. Violations trigger an excise tax under IRC Section 4975 - initially 15% of the amount involved, rising to 100% if not corrected - and can be remedied only through statutory or administrative exemptions.", "keyPoints": ["An ERISA fiduciary is anyone exercising discretionary control over plan assets/administration OR rendering investment advice for a fee; the test is functional, not just titular.", "The ERISA prudent-expert standard requires acting solely in participants' interest, for the exclusive purpose of providing benefits, with professional care/skill, and diversifying to minimize large losses.", "Section 404(c) shifts liability for investment RESULTS to participants only in participant-directed plans that give real control and a broad range of options.", "A 404(c) 'broad range' generally means at least three diversified alternatives with materially different risk/return characteristics that let a participant build a diversified portfolio.", "404(c) plans must let participants change investment allocations at least quarterly (more often for volatile options).", "Even under 404(c), the fiduciary always remains responsible for prudently SELECTING and MONITORING the investment menu offered.", "ERISA does NOT statutorily require an Investment Policy Statement, but maintaining and following one is a strong best practice and evidence of prudent process.", "The IPS documents objectives, risk tolerance, permitted asset classes, and selection/monitoring/replacement criteria; it is a guideline, not the plan document itself.", "A 'party in interest' (ERISA) / 'disqualified person' (IRC) includes fiduciaries, service providers, the employer/sponsor, unions, and certain owners and relatives.", "Section 406 prohibited transactions include sale/exchange/lease of property, lending or extending credit, furnishing goods/services/facilities, and transfer of plan assets to a party in interest.", "Section 406(b) separately prohibits fiduciary self-dealing and acting on both sides of a transaction (conflict of interest).", "Prohibited transactions carry an IRC 4975 excise tax of 15% of the amount involved, rising to 100% if not timely corrected.", "ERISA generally requires that plan assets be held in trust and diversified; failure to diversify without clear prudence is itself a breach.", "Reasonable, necessary services provided for reasonable compensation can qualify for statutory exemptions from the prohibited-transaction rules."], "formulas": ["Prohibited-transaction excise tax = 15% x amount involved (initial), then 100% x amount involved if not corrected within the taxable period.", "404(c) 'broad range' minimum = at least 3 diversified investment alternatives with materially different risk/return characteristics."], "mnemonics": ["Prudent-expert duties = 'DEEP D': Diversify, Exclusive-purpose, Expert care/skill, Prudence, Documents (follow plan docs consistent with ERISA).", "406 prohibited transactions = 'SELF': Sale/exchange/lease of property; Extending credit or lending money; Lending goods / Furnishing services or facilities; and Fiduciary self-dealing (406(b)).", "404(c) relief = 'Control + Choice': participants get real Control and a broad range of Choices, so they own the RESULTS - but you still own the MENU."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming 404(c) relieves the fiduciary of ALL liability - it never relieves the duty to prudently select and monitor the plan's investment lineup.", "Thinking ERISA legally REQUIRES an IPS - it does not; it is best practice and evidence of prudence, not a statutory mandate.", "Confusing 404(c) (participant-directed liability relief) with the diversification duty - offering a broad menu does not excuse imprudent option selection.", "Forgetting that a prohibited transaction is barred even if it is fair to the plan or profitable; good intentions and fair pricing do not cure a 406 violation absent an exemption.", "Mixing up the excise-tax tiers: it starts at 15% of the amount involved and escalates to 100% only if the transaction is not corrected.", "Believing only the person with the fiduciary title is bound - ERISA uses a functional test, so anyone giving investment advice for a fee to the plan can be a fiduciary."]},
  "rec-accounts": {"summary": "Special types of accounts appear on the Series 65 because each carries distinct tax treatment, contribution rules, and control features an adviser must match to a client's goal. The three most heavily tested are education-funding vehicles (529 plans and Coverdell ESAs), custodial accounts for minors (UGMA/UTMA), and health savings accounts (HSAs). All three offer some form of tax advantage, but they differ sharply in who controls the money, what the money may be spent on, and what happens if it is used for the wrong purpose.\n\nEducation accounts grow tax-deferred and distribute tax-free when used for qualified education expenses. A 529 plan is state-sponsored, has very high aggregate contribution limits, no income limit on the donor, and lets the account owner (not the beneficiary) retain control and change the beneficiary. A Coverdell ESA is capped at $2,000 per beneficiary per year, phases out at higher incomes, and must generally be used by age 30. UGMA/UTMA custodial accounts are irrevocable gifts to a minor: the custodian manages the assets but the minor is the legal owner, and control transfers to the (former) minor at the age of majority. HSAs pair with a high-deductible health plan (HDHP) and are triple tax-advantaged - deductible contributions, tax-free growth, and tax-free qualified medical withdrawals.\n\nOn the exam, watch the control/ownership distinction (owner controls a 529; the minor owns an UGMA/UTMA), the penalty mechanics (10% penalty on the earnings portion of nonqualified 529 and Coverdell distributions, but 20% on nonqualified HSA distributions before age 65), and the funding source (an HSA requires HDHP coverage). Numeric traps cluster on contribution caps and phase-outs.", "keyPoints": ["529 plan: account OWNER retains control, can change the beneficiary to another family member, and can reclaim funds (subject to tax and penalty on earnings); the beneficiary never controls the assets.", "529 contributions are made with after-tax dollars (no federal deduction), grow tax-deferred, and are federally tax-free when used for qualified higher-education expenses (and up to $10,000/yr for K-12 tuition).", "529 plans have no income limits on the donor and very high aggregate limits; contributions qualify for the annual gift-tax exclusion, with 5-year gift-tax 'front-loading' (superfunding) allowed.", "Coverdell ESA is limited to $2,000 per beneficiary per year (aggregate across all contributors), has income phase-outs for contributors, funds must generally be contributed before the beneficiary turns 18 and used by age 30, and can pay K-12 as well as higher-education expenses.", "Nonqualified 529 and Coverdell distributions: the EARNINGS portion is taxed as ordinary income plus a 10% penalty; the return of principal/contributions is never taxed or penalized.", "UGMA/UTMA is an irrevocable gift to a minor; the minor is the legal/beneficial owner, and the custodian manages assets for the minor's benefit only.", "UTMA allows a broader range of property (real estate, art, intellectual property) and lets control extend to a later age than UGMA; UGMA is limited mainly to financial assets (cash, securities, insurance).", "Custodial account control passes to the former minor at the age of majority set by state law; there is one custodian and one minor per account, and the custodian cannot be changed arbitrarily.", "The 'kiddie tax' may tax a minor's unearned investment income above an annual threshold at the parents'/higher rates.", "HSA eligibility requires enrollment in a qualifying high-deductible health plan (HDHP) and no other disqualifying coverage; you cannot contribute once enrolled in Medicare.", "HSAs are triple tax-advantaged: contributions are deductible (above-the-line), growth is tax-deferred, and qualified medical distributions are tax-free.", "HSA nonqualified withdrawals before age 65 are taxed as ordinary income plus a 20% penalty; after age 65, nonqualified withdrawals are taxed as ordinary income but the penalty no longer applies (like a traditional IRA).", "HSA funds roll over year to year (no 'use it or lose it') and the account is owned by the individual, not the employer - it is portable.", "A 529 owned by a parent or the student is generally treated as a parental asset for federal financial-aid (FAFSA) purposes, assessed more favorably than a student-owned asset."], "formulas": ["Nonqualified 529/Coverdell tax = (ordinary income tax on earnings portion) + (10% penalty on earnings portion); principal is untaxed.", "Nonqualified HSA distribution before 65 = ordinary income tax on full withdrawal + 20% penalty; at/after 65 = ordinary income tax only (no penalty).", "Earnings portion of a distribution = Distribution amount x (Total account earnings / Total account value)."], "mnemonics": ["HSA is 'triple tax-free': Deduct going in, Grow inside, Withdraw out (for medical) - D-G-W.", "Coverdell = 'Cap of two' ($2,000/year) and 'done by thirty' (age 30 use-by).", "529 = 'owner rules' (owner keeps control); UTMA = 'minor owns' (irrevocable to the child)."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing who controls a 529 (the owner) with who owns an UGMA/UTMA (the minor) - these are opposite.", "Assuming the HSA penalty is 10% - it is 20% before age 65 (10% is the 529/Coverdell figure).", "Thinking a nonqualified education distribution taxes the whole withdrawal - only the EARNINGS portion is taxed/penalized; contributions come back tax-free.", "Believing HSA funds expire annually - they roll over indefinitely; that is the FSA rule, not the HSA rule.", "Forgetting that an HSA requires HDHP coverage and that Medicare enrollment ends eligibility to contribute.", "Overstating the Coverdell limit - it is $2,000 per beneficiary total per year, not per contributor, and phases out at higher incomes.", "Assuming UGMA and UTMA are identical - UTMA permits a wider range of assets and a later transfer age."]},
  "rec-ownership": {"summary": "Estate planning on the Series 65 focuses on how property is titled, how it transfers at death, and the legal vehicles that control that transfer. The core distinction is whether an asset passes automatically (by operation of law or by contract/beneficiary designation) or must go through probate under a will (or intestacy if there is no will). Assets that transfer outside probate include jointly held property with survivorship rights (JTWROS and tenancy by the entirety), TOD/POD accounts, and any account with a valid beneficiary designation (retirement accounts, annuities, life insurance).\n\nOwnership forms differ in survivorship and control. In Joint Tenancy With Right of Survivorship (JTWROS), a deceased owner's share passes automatically to the surviving joint owner(s), bypassing probate and the will. Tenants in Common (TIC) has NO survivorship: each owner holds a divisible (not necessarily equal) fractional interest that passes to that owner's estate/heirs. Tenancy by the Entirety is a survivorship form available only to married couples in states that recognize it, adding creditor protection because neither spouse can unilaterally sever or encumber the property.\n\nTransfer-on-death (TOD) for securities/brokerage accounts and Payable-on-death (POD) for bank accounts let a sole owner name a beneficiary who receives the assets at death without probate; the owner retains full control while alive and the beneficiary has no rights until death. Beneficiary designations on retirement plans, IRAs, annuities, and life insurance override the will, which is why keeping them current is critical. Trusts (revocable/living vs. irrevocable) can avoid probate, provide management and privacy, and support incapacity planning; a will controls only probate assets and, unlike a funded living trust, becomes public. A QDRO is a court order dividing qualified retirement plan benefits in divorce/separation, letting benefits be paid to an alternate payee (spouse, ex-spouse, child, or dependent). Donor-advised funds give the donor an immediate charitable deduction upon irrevocable contribution while allowing later advisory recommendations on grants and investments.", "keyPoints": ["JTWROS: deceased owner's interest passes automatically to surviving owner(s) outside probate; overrides the will.", "Tenants in common (TIC): NO right of survivorship; each owner's fractional share (can be unequal) passes to that owner's estate/heirs and goes through probate.", "Tenancy by the entirety: survivorship ownership limited to married couples (where recognized); adds creditor protection because one spouse cannot sever or encumber alone.", "TOD (transfer on death) applies to brokerage/securities accounts; POD (payable on death) applies to bank accounts; both avoid probate and give the beneficiary no rights until the owner dies.", "Beneficiary designations on IRAs, qualified plans, annuities, and life insurance pass by contract and OVERRIDE the will; probate is bypassed.", "A will directs only probate (individually owned, non-beneficiary, non-survivorship) assets; dying without a will = intestate, distributed by state law.", "Revocable (living) trust: grantor retains control, avoids probate, provides privacy and incapacity management, but assets remain in the taxable estate and offer no creditor protection.", "Irrevocable trust: grantor gives up control; can remove assets from the taxable estate and provide creditor/asset protection.", "A funded living trust avoids probate and stays private; a will becomes a public record when probated.", "QDRO (qualified domestic relations order): a court order used in divorce/separation to divide QUALIFIED plan (ERISA) benefits to an alternate payee (spouse, ex-spouse, child, dependent). IRAs are NOT divided by a QDRO.", "Donor-advised fund (DAF): contribution is IRREVOCABLE and gives an immediate charitable income-tax deduction; donor may only advise (recommend, not control) future grants and investments.", "Probate = court process to validate a will and settle an estate; it is public, can be costly and slow, and only touches probate assets.", "Titling/beneficiary designations control disposition regardless of what the will says; a will cannot redirect a JTWROS or TOD/beneficiary asset."], "mnemonics": ["'Survivorship Skips probate' - JTWROS and tenancy by the entirety bypass the will.", "'Beneficiary Beats the will' - contract designations (IRA, annuity, life insurance, TOD/POD) override the will.", "'QDRO = Qualified plans only' - the Q links divorce orders to ERISA/qualified plans, not IRAs.", "'DAF = Deduction now, Advise later' - immediate deduction, irrevocable gift, advisory-only grants."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming a will overrides a beneficiary designation or a TOD/JTWROS title - it does not; titling and contract designations control.", "Confusing TOD (securities/brokerage) with POD (bank accounts); also assuming the beneficiary has rights before the owner dies (they do not).", "Thinking tenants in common has survivorship - it does not; only JTWROS and tenancy by the entirety do.", "Believing a revocable living trust removes assets from the taxable estate or provides creditor protection - it does neither; only an irrevocable trust can.", "Assuming a QDRO is used to divide an IRA - IRAs are split by a divorce decree/transfer incident to divorce, not a QDRO; QDROs apply to qualified/ERISA plans.", "Forgetting that donor-advised fund contributions are irrevocable and grants are advisory only - the sponsoring charity has legal control and the deduction is taken in the year of contribution.", "Assuming tenancy by the entirety is available to any co-owners - it is limited to married couples in states that recognize it."]},
  "rec-trading": {"summary": "Trading securities on the Series 65 tests the mechanics and vocabulary of the secondary market: how orders are entered, who the players are, and what trading actually costs the client. A quote consists of a bid (the highest price a buyer will pay) and an ask/offer (the lowest price a seller will accept); the difference is the bid/ask spread, an implicit cost of trading. Order types control price and timing: a market order guarantees execution (not price), a limit order guarantees price or better (not execution), and a stop order becomes a live order only once a trigger price is touched. Accounts come in two flavors: cash accounts (pay in full) and margin accounts (borrow part of the purchase from the broker-dealer). Short sales, where a client sells borrowed stock hoping to buy it back lower, must occur in a margin account.\n\nThe key institutional distinction is capacity: a broker-dealer acting as agent (broker) arranges a trade between two other parties and charges a commission, while a broker-dealer acting as principal (dealer) trades from its own inventory and is compensated through a markup (on a sale to the client) or markdown (on a purchase from the client). Market makers are dealers that continuously post two-sided quotes and stand ready to buy and sell a security, providing liquidity; exchanges provide the centralized venue and rules; custodians hold client cash and securities for safekeeping and settlement.\n\nCosts of trading include explicit charges (commissions on agency trades, markups/markdowns on principal trades) and implicit costs (the bid/ask spread and market impact). Over all of this sits the best execution obligation: a firm must seek the most favorable terms reasonably available for a customer order, considering price, speed, and likelihood of execution, and cannot let arrangements like payment for order flow (compensation a firm receives for routing orders to a particular market maker) compromise that duty.", "keyPoints": ["Bid = highest price a buyer will pay; ask/offer = lowest price a seller will accept; the difference is the bid/ask spread, an implicit cost of trading.", "A market order guarantees execution but not price; a limit order guarantees price (or better) but not execution.", "A buy limit and a sell stop are both placed BELOW the current market; a sell limit and a buy stop are both placed ABOVE the current market.", "A stop order is dormant until the market touches the stop (trigger) price; it then becomes a market order (stop) or a limit order (stop-limit).", "Agency (broker) capacity: the BD arranges the trade for others and charges a disclosed commission; principal (dealer) capacity: the BD trades from its own inventory and earns a markup/markdown.", "A single trade is either agency or principal for the customer, not both; the confirmation must disclose capacity.", "Market makers post continuous two-sided (bid and ask) quotes and provide liquidity by trading their own inventory as principal.", "Custodians hold client cash and securities for safekeeping; using a qualified custodian is central to the adviser custody rule.", "Short sales must be conducted in a margin account because the seller borrows the security; the position is closed by buying it back (covering).", "Cash account = pay in full by settlement; margin account = borrow part of the purchase price from the BD, subject to Reg T and interest.", "Payment for order flow is compensation a BD receives for routing customer orders to a specific market maker; it must be disclosed and must never override best execution.", "Best execution requires seeking the most favorable terms reasonably available, weighing price, speed, and likelihood of execution - not merely the venue that pays the firm.", "Markups/markdowns apply to principal trades; commissions apply to agency trades - the two labels are mutually exclusive for a given fill."], "formulas": ["Bid/ask spread = Ask price - Bid price", "Principal sale to client: total cost = market price + markup; Principal purchase from client: net proceeds = market price - markdown", "Agency trade: total cost to buyer = execution price + commission (proceeds to seller = execution price - commission)"], "mnemonics": ["Order placement: 'BLiSS' - Buy Limit and Sell Stop go below; the other two (Sell Limit, Buy Stop) go above.", "Capacity pay: Agent earns a Commission; Dealer/principal earns a marKup - 'Dealer = marKdown/marKup, from my own stocK.'", "Limit = price Locked, execution not guaranteed; Market = Motion guaranteed, price not."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing which side a stop protects: a long position is protected by a SELL stop placed below the market, not a buy stop.", "Assuming a market order gets a specific price - it only guarantees the trade happens; the fill price can move (slippage).", "Calling a principal-trade charge a 'commission' - principal trades carry a markup/markdown, and a trade cannot be both agency and principal for the same customer.", "Thinking payment for order flow is prohibited - it is permitted but must be disclosed and cannot compromise best execution.", "Believing best execution means only best price - it also weighs speed and likelihood of execution.", "Forgetting that short selling requires a margin account (you must borrow the shares); it cannot be done in a cash account.", "Mixing up custodian (safekeeping) with market maker (liquidity provider) or with broker-dealer capacity."]},
  "rec-performance": {"summary": "Portfolio performance measures let an adviser evaluate how an investment or portfolio has performed and compare it fairly against alternatives. The core distinction on the exam is between measures that describe raw return and those that adjust for risk, timing of cash flows, taxes, or inflation. Time-weighted return isolates the manager's skill by removing the distorting effect of client deposits and withdrawals, whereas dollar-weighted return (an internal rate of return, IRR) reflects the investor's actual experience because it weights periods by how much money was invested. Because clients control the timing of contributions, GIPS and NASAA-consistent performance reporting favor time-weighted return to judge a manager.\n\nReturns can be expressed many ways. Holding period return captures total gain (income plus appreciation) over the entire time an asset is held without annualizing; annualized (and its compound cousin, the geometric/CAGR) return puts returns on a comparable per-year basis. Total return combines income and capital appreciation, while current yield looks only at annual income relative to current price. Risk-adjusted measures such as the Sharpe ratio divide excess return (return minus the risk-free rate) by standard deviation, letting an adviser see whether higher return came from skill or simply from taking more risk.\n\nAfter-tax and inflation-adjusted (real) returns strip away taxes and purchasing-power erosion so the investor sees what was actually kept. The real (inflation-adjusted) return is approximately the nominal return minus the inflation rate. Expected return is a probability-weighted forecast of possible outcomes, not a historical figure. Finally, a relevant benchmark must match the portfolio's asset class, style, and risk so the comparison is meaningful: the S&P 500 for large-cap U.S. equities, the Russell 2000 for small-cap, the Bloomberg U.S. Aggregate for investment-grade bonds, and the appropriate index for international or sector holdings.", "keyPoints": ["Time-weighted return removes the effect of client cash flows (deposits/withdrawals) and is the standard for evaluating a manager's skill.", "Dollar-weighted return equals the internal rate of return (IRR); it weights each period by dollars invested and reflects the investor's actual outcome, including their timing decisions.", "Holding period return = (income + (end value - begin value)) / begin value, measured over the whole holding period and NOT annualized.", "Total return = income (interest/dividends) + capital appreciation, expressed as a percentage of the amount invested.", "Current yield = annual income / current market price; for a bond it is annual coupon / current price and moves inversely to price.", "Current yield ignores capital gain/loss and time to maturity, so it is not the same as yield to maturity (YTM) or total return.", "The Sharpe ratio = (portfolio return - risk-free rate) / standard deviation; higher is better and it rewards return per unit of total risk.", "Real (inflation-adjusted) return is approximately the nominal return minus the inflation rate.", "After-tax return reflects returns net of taxes; municipal bond interest is generally federally tax-free, which raises its after-tax return relative to a taxable bond of the same yield.", "Expected return is a probability-weighted average of possible outcomes (a forecast), not a realized historical result.", "A relevant benchmark must match the portfolio's asset class, market-cap, style, and risk: S&P 500 (large-cap U.S. equity), Russell 2000 (small-cap), Bloomberg U.S. Aggregate Bond (investment-grade bonds).", "IRR / dollar-weighted return is the discount rate that sets the present value of all cash inflows and outflows (including the ending value) to zero.", "Risk-adjusted return matters because two portfolios with the same raw return are not equal if one took far more risk to get there."], "formulas": ["Holding Period Return = [Income + (Ending Value - Beginning Value)] / Beginning Value", "Total Return = (Income + Capital Appreciation) / Beginning Value", "Current Yield (bond) = Annual Coupon Interest / Current Market Price", "Sharpe Ratio = (Portfolio Return - Risk-Free Rate) / Standard Deviation of Portfolio", "Real (Inflation-Adjusted) Return ≈ Nominal Return - Inflation Rate", "Approx. After-Tax Return = Nominal Return × (1 - Tax Rate) for fully taxable ordinary income", "Dollar-Weighted Return = IRR (rate where NPV of all cash flows including ending value = 0)"], "mnemonics": ["'Time judges the Talent': Time-weighted return judges the manager (Talent) because it ignores client cash-flow timing.", "'Dollars follow the Dude': Dollar-weighted (IRR) follows the investor's own contribution timing and reflects their actual result.", "Sharpe = 'excess Split by std dev' — numerator is return above the risk-free rate, denominator is standard deviation."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing current yield with yield to maturity or total return — current yield ignores any capital gain/loss and the pull to par at maturity.", "Assuming dollar-weighted return is best for judging a manager — it is not, because it is distorted by client-controlled deposits and withdrawals; time-weighted is the correct choice.", "Thinking a higher raw return automatically means better performance — without adjusting for risk (e.g., Sharpe ratio), the comparison can be misleading.", "Comparing a small-cap or bond portfolio to the S&P 500 — a benchmark must match asset class, style, and risk to be relevant.", "Treating expected return as a historical/realized figure — it is a probability-weighted forecast.", "Forgetting that real return subtracts inflation and that after-tax return subtracts taxes; a high nominal return can be a poor real, after-tax return.", "Annualizing a holding period return by mistake — HPR is the total return over the entire period held, not per year."]},
  "law-ia": {"summary": "The regulation of investment advisers rests on a two-tier system split between the SEC (federal-covered advisers) and the states. An investment adviser is defined by the three-part test (USA §401 / IAA §202(a)(11)): a person who (1) for compensation, (2) is in the business of, (3) giving advice about securities. All three prongs must be met. Certain professionals — lawyers, accountants, teachers, and engineers (the 'LATE' group) whose advice is solely incidental and not separately compensated — plus banks, broker-dealers whose advice is solely incidental and who receive no special compensation, and publishers of bona fide general-circulation publications, are excluded from the federal definition.\n\nWhether an adviser registers with the SEC or the states turns on assets under management (AUM). Under IAA §203A, advisers with $100 million or more in regulatory AUM (or that advise registered investment companies) generally must register with the SEC and are 'federal-covered.' A mid-sized adviser ($25M–<$100M) registers with the state if its home state requires registration and would subject it to examination; below $25M, registration is at the state level. Federal-covered advisers do not register with states, but states may still require notice filing — essentially filing a copy of Form ADV, paying state fees, and filing a consent to service of process — and retain anti-fraud authority.\n\nRegistration is accomplished by filing Form ADV through the IARD system. Part 1 is regulatory/check-the-box data; Part 2A is the client-facing 'brochure' written in plain English; Part 2B is the brochure supplement covering individual advisory personnel. State registration under USA §201–204 becomes effective at noon on the 30th day after a complete filing absent a stop order or pending proceeding. Post-registration duties include maintaining books and records, delivering the brochure, meeting financial/net-worth and bonding requirements for custody or discretion, and supervising investment adviser representatives (IARs).\n\nA separate lighter-touch regime covers Exempt Reporting Advisers (ERAs): advisers relying on the venture-capital-fund adviser exemption or the private-fund adviser exemption (solely private funds with under $150M in U.S. private-fund AUM) do not fully register but must file portions of Form ADV Part 1 as 'reporting' advisers and remain subject to anti-fraud rules. IAR supervision is a core state-level obligation, requiring reasonable written supervisory procedures and a designated supervisor.", "keyPoints": ["The three-prong IA definition (Advice + Business + Compensation — 'ABC'): a person must, for COMPENSATION, be in the BUSINESS of ADVISING others about SECURITIES; all three must be present.", "Excluded from the federal IA definition ('LATE' + others): Lawyers, Accountants, Teachers, Engineers whose advice is solely incidental and not separately compensated; plus Banks, Broker-Dealers (incidental advice, no special comp), and bona fide general-circulation Publishers.", "$100M+ regulatory AUM (or advising a registered investment company) = federal-covered adviser, must register with the SEC under IAA §203A; below $25M = state registration.", "Mid-sized adviser ($25M–<$100M AUM) registers with the STATE if the home state requires registration and would subject it to examination; otherwise it registers with the SEC.", "Federal-covered advisers are not registered by states, but states may require NOTICE FILING (a Form ADV copy + fees + consent to service of process) and always keep anti-fraud jurisdiction.", "Form ADV: Part 1 = regulatory/check-the-box data; Part 2A = the client 'brochure' (plain English); Part 2B = brochure supplement on individual advisory personnel.", "Brochure delivery (SEC Rule 204-3 / NASAA rule): deliver Part 2 at or before entering the advisory contract; annually, deliver an updated brochure or a summary of material changes within 120 days of fiscal year-end.", "State registration under USA §201–204 becomes effective at noon on the 30th day after a complete filing, absent a stop order or pending proceeding.", "Books and records must generally be kept 5 years by state-registered advisers (first 2 years in an easily accessible/on-site location); SEC-registered advisers keep records per SEC Rule 204-2.", "Custody triggers heightened net worth/surprise-audit rules; discretionary authority also triggers minimum net worth or a surety bond — states typically require a bond if net worth falls below the minimum.", "Exempt Reporting Advisers (ERAs): venture-capital-fund advisers and private-fund advisers (only private funds, <$150M U.S. private-fund AUM) don't register but must file parts of Form ADV Part 1.", "IAR supervision (NASAA model rule): firms must adopt reasonable written supervisory procedures and designate a qualified supervisor; failure to supervise is itself a violation.", "An IAR is an individual (natural person) associated with an adviser who gives advice, manages accounts, or solicits/supervises; IARs generally must register in any state where they maintain a place of business.", "The private-fund adviser exemption is capped at less than $150M of private-fund AUM managed in the U.S.; reaching $150M forces full SEC registration."], "mnemonics": ["'ABC' for the investment-adviser test: Advice (about securities) + Business + Compensation — all three required.", "'LATE' for the incidental-professional exclusions: Lawyers, Accountants, Teachers, Engineers.", "'100 / 25' AUM rule: $100M+ = SEC (federal-covered); under $25M = state; the $25M–$100M band is the mid-sized 'switch' zone.", "'30 at noon' — state registration is effective at noon on the 30th day after filing."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing the broker-dealer exclusion (advice must be solely INCIDENTAL to brokerage AND receive NO special compensation — if a BD charges a separate advisory fee or wrap fee, the exclusion is lost).", "Assuming federal-covered advisers escape state authority entirely — states can still require notice filing and always enforce anti-fraud provisions.", "Mixing up the AUM thresholds: $100M is the core SEC line, not $110M; the buffer that lets an adviser stay SEC-registered down to $90M (and requires SEC registration at $110M) is a separate 'hysteresis' feature, but the threshold tested is $100M.", "Thinking ERAs are fully unregistered — they still must FILE portions of Form ADV Part 1 and are subject to anti-fraud rules.", "Forgetting the annual brochure requirement: an updated brochure or summary of material changes must be delivered within 120 days of fiscal year-end — not 90.", "Treating a publisher's newsletter as advisory activity — a bona fide, regular, general-circulation publication that gives only impersonal advice is excluded; losing 'regular' or 'general circulation' or giving personalized advice destroys the exclusion.", "Confusing an IAR (a natural person) with the investment adviser firm (the entity that registers and files Form ADV).", "Assuming custody and discretion have the same net-worth trigger — custody generally requires a higher minimum net worth (or bond) than discretion."]},
  "law-iar": {"summary": "An investment adviser representative (IAR) is a natural person — not a firm — associated with an investment adviser who does one or more of the following: makes recommendations or renders advice about securities; manages client accounts or portfolios; determines which advice should be given; solicits, offers, or negotiates for the sale of advisory services; or supervises employees who do any of these. Clerical and purely administrative staff are excluded. Under the Uniform Securities Act (USA § 401) the term is defined at the state level, while SEC Rule 203A-3 gives the federal definition used to allocate IARs between state and federal-covered advisers.\n\nRegistration turns on WHERE the IAR has a place of business, not on the size of the firm's AUM. An IAR of a federal-covered adviser still registers at the STATE level in any state where the IAR maintains a place of business. The federal-covered adviser itself registers only with the SEC (states cannot require its registration), but the individuals who work out of an office in a state are subject to that state's IAR registration, recordkeeping of Form U4, and the state's antifraud authority.\n\nRegistration is accomplished by filing Form U4 through the IARD/CRD system, together with any required fee and exam qualifications (typically the Series 65, or Series 66 plus Series 7). Registration of an IAR is only effective while that person is employed by or associated with a registered or federal-covered adviser — it is not portable and does not stand alone. When the association ends, both the IAR and the adviser generally have notice obligations, and post-registration duties include keeping the U4 current by amendment (usually within 30 days) for material changes such as new disclosure events, address, or name changes. It is unlawful under USA § 206 to represent that registration constitutes the Administrator's approval of the person or a finding as to the accuracy of any filing.", "keyPoints": ["An IAR must be a natural person (an individual); a firm or entity can be an investment adviser but never an IAR.", "USA § 401 defines the IAR at the state level; SEC Rule 203A-3 provides the federal definition and allocates individuals between state and federal-covered advisers.", "Functions that make someone an IAR: giving securities advice/recommendations, managing accounts, determining what advice is given, soliciting/offering/negotiating advisory services, or supervising anyone who does these.", "Purely clerical or ministerial (administrative) personnel are NOT IARs.", "IARs register at the STATE level — even IARs of a federal-covered adviser register in each state where they have a place of business.", "The trigger for state IAR registration is having a 'place of business' in the state, not the adviser's AUM.", "Form U4 is the registration form; it is filed electronically through the IARD/CRD system with the applicable fee and exam qualification.", "An IAR's registration is effective only while associated with a registered or federal-covered adviser; it lapses when the association ends and is not portable between firms.", "Material changes to Form U4 (e.g., new disclosure/disciplinary events, address, name) must be amended promptly — generally within 30 days.", "USA §§ 201–204 cover the registration requirement, procedure, and the Administrator's power to deny, suspend, or revoke registrations; USA § 206 prohibits representing that registration implies the Administrator's approval.", "NASAA has adopted model rules covering IA/IAR minimum financial (net worth/bonding) requirements, recordkeeping, custody, and dishonest or unethical business practices; state Administrators may also impose supervisory obligations on advisory firms.", "Registration of an IAR does not imply that the Administrator has approved the person's qualifications or found the disclosures accurate — representing otherwise is prohibited under USA § 206.", "A person soliciting advisory clients for a fee generally must be registered as an IAR even if they give no specific securities advice themselves."], "mnemonics": ["IAR functions — 'RAMSS': Recommends/renders advice, Manages accounts, determines Advice to give, Solicits/offers advisory services, Supervises those who do.", "'IARs register in STATES, advisers register by SIZE' — individuals go to the state where they have a place of business; the firm's SEC-vs-state status depends on AUM."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming an IAR of a federal-covered (SEC-registered) adviser registers with the SEC — the IAR still registers at the STATE level where they have a place of business.", "Thinking a firm or entity can be an 'IAR' — only a natural person can be an IAR.", "Confusing the trigger: state IAR registration hinges on a PLACE OF BUSINESS in the state, not on the firm's assets under management or the de minimis client count.", "Believing clerical/administrative staff must register — they are excluded unless they perform advisory functions.", "Assuming an IAR's registration is portable — it is tied to the current firm and must be re-established (new/transfer U4) upon changing firms.", "Overlooking that soliciting advisory clients for compensation makes someone an IAR even without giving specific investment advice.", "Thinking registration is an endorsement — representing that registration means the Administrator approved the IAR or verified the filing is unlawful under USA § 206."]},
  "law-bd": {"summary": "Broker-dealers are the intermediaries of the securities markets, and their regulation spans the Uniform Securities Act (USA) at the state level and the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (SEA) at the federal level. Under USA Section 401, a broker-dealer is any person engaged in the business of effecting transactions in securities for the accounts of others (broker) or for its own account (dealer). The definition deliberately EXCLUDES agents, issuers, and banks/savings institutions/trust companies. It also excludes a person with no place of business in a state who deals only with existing customers who are temporarily present, or exclusively with institutional/other BD clients — the classic 'snowbird' and institutional exclusions.\n\nRelated defined actors carry precise meanings. An underwriter (Securities Act of 1933 Section 2(a)(11)) is any person who purchases from an issuer with a view to distribution, offers or sells for an issuer in connection with a distribution, or participates in that undertaking; the definition expressly excludes those earning only the usual and customary seller's/dealer's commission. A market maker holds itself out as willing to buy and sell a security for its own account on a regular and continuous basis at quoted prices — providing liquidity. An associated person of a broker-dealer (SEA Section 3(a)(18)) is broadly any partner, officer, director, branch manager, or employee, plus persons controlling or controlled by the BD — though purely clerical/ministerial personnel are generally excluded for registration purposes.\n\nRegistration is the operational core. A BD must register in a state where it has a place of business or transacts business (unless an exclusion or exemption applies). Federal-covered securities and federal registration under the SEA (via SRO membership, typically FINRA) do not eliminate state BD registration. Registration is effected by filing (Form BD via the CRD/Web CRD system), paying fees, and often meeting minimum net capital and surety bond requirements. Post-registration duties include recordkeeping, financial reporting, supervision of agents, and keeping the registration current; registrations expire December 31 and must be renewed annually.", "keyPoints": ["USA § 401: a broker-dealer effects securities transactions for others' accounts (broker) or its own account (dealer) as a business.", "Excluded from the BD definition under the USA: agents, issuers, and banks/savings institutions/trust companies.", "A person with NO place of business in the state is NOT a BD there if it deals only with existing customers temporarily in the state, or exclusively with other BDs and institutional clients (issuers, banks, insurers, investment companies, registered advisers, etc.).", "Securities Act of 1933 § 2(a)(11): an underwriter buys from the issuer with a view to distribution, or offers/sells for the issuer in a distribution; a person earning only the usual seller's/dealer's commission is NOT an underwriter.", "A market maker stands ready to buy and sell a security for its own account on a regular/continuous basis at quoted (two-sided) prices, supplying market liquidity; it acts as a dealer (principal).", "SEA § 3(a)(18): an 'associated person' of a BD includes partners, officers, directors, branch managers, employees, and persons controlling/controlled by the BD; purely clerical or ministerial staff are generally excluded.", "State BD registration is required where the firm has a place of business OR transacts business, unless an exclusion or exemption applies; federal (SEA) registration does not remove the state requirement.", "Federal BD registration under the SEA generally requires membership in an SRO (typically FINRA).", "BD registration is effected by filing Form BD through the CRD (Central Registration Depository / Web CRD) system, paying fees, and meeting any net capital / surety bond conditions.", "Post-registration requirements: maintain books and records, file financial reports, supervise agents, keep the application current by amending material changes, and renew annually.", "State registrations expire December 31 each year and must be renewed regardless of the original effective date.", "When a BD registration is effective, its agents' registrations that were pending become effective; if a BD's registration is withdrawn or revoked, its agents can no longer transact business.", "A firm dealing only with existing clients while those clients are on vacation in another state (the 'snowbird' scenario) need not register in that state.", "Broker (agency, for others) vs. dealer (principal, own account): a market maker acts in a dealer/principal capacity."], "mnemonics": ["BD exclusions from the USA definition = 'A-I-B': Agents, Issuers, Banks (and savings institutions/trust companies).", "Underwriter test = 'issuer + distribution': you buy FROM the issuer with a view to distribute, or sell FOR the issuer in a distribution — mere resale for the usual commission does not count.", "Renewals: 'December 31 — everybody resets,' regardless of when you first registered."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing the USA agent exclusion: an AGENT is excluded from the BD definition, but the individual is still separately regulated as an agent — the exclusion does not mean 'unregulated.'", "Assuming a no-place-of-business firm is always exempt: the institutional/existing-customer exclusions are narrow — soliciting new retail clients in a state triggers registration even without an office there.", "Thinking a person who merely resells securities is an underwriter — earning only the usual seller's or dealer's commission specifically excludes you.", "Believing federal (SEC/FINRA) registration substitutes for state BD registration — the two coexist; a BD transacting business in a state still registers there.", "Assuming every employee of a BD is an 'associated person' requiring registration — purely clerical/ministerial staff are generally excluded.", "Forgetting that all state registrations expire December 31 — candidates wrongly assume a one-year anniversary date from the effective date."]},
  "law-bda": {"summary": "Under the Uniform Securities Act (USA), an \"agent\" is defined in Section 401 as any INDIVIDUAL (a natural person) who represents a broker-dealer or an issuer in effecting or attempting to effect transactions in securities. The key insight is that only individuals are agents: the broker-dealer entity itself is never an agent, and a corporate or partnership entity is never classified as an agent. Clerical and administrative personnel who do not solicit, take orders, or effect securities transactions are excluded from the definition, so they need not register. Certain individuals who represent issuers are also excluded (for example, those effecting transactions in specified exempt securities or in exempt transactions, or those representing an issuer with no sales compensation), meaning they are not agents and need not register as such.\n\nIf an individual falls within the definition of an agent, it is unlawful to transact business in a state unless the agent is registered in that state OR is exempt. Registration is tied to a single employing broker-dealer: a broker-dealer may not employ an unregistered agent, and an agent may not be employed by (or effect transactions for) an unregistered broker-dealer. An agent's registration is not effective during any period when the agent is not associated with a registered broker-dealer; if the agent changes firms, the old employer, the new employer, AND the agent must all promptly notify the Administrator.\n\nRegistration is accomplished by filing Form U4 through the Central Registration Depository (CRD). Form U4 collects personal and professional history and requires disclosure of criminal, regulatory, civil-judicial, financial (bankruptcy, unsatisfied judgments/liens), and customer-complaint events. Registration generally requires the application (consent to service of process is part of it), payment of fees, any surety bond the Administrator requires (bonding may be imposed on agents who have custody of or discretionary authority over client assets; net-capital requirements, by contrast, apply to the broker-dealer firm rather than to individual agents), and passing the appropriate qualifying examination(s). Once effective, the agent has an ongoing post-registration duty to keep Form U4 current by filing amendments promptly (generally within 30 days) whenever the information becomes materially inaccurate.", "keyPoints": ["An agent under USA § 401 is an INDIVIDUAL (natural person) who represents a broker-dealer or issuer in effecting or attempting to effect securities transactions.", "A broker-dealer (the firm/entity) is NEVER an agent; 'agent' applies only to human beings representing the firm.", "Clerical and administrative employees who do not solicit or effect transactions are NOT agents and need not register.", "An individual representing an ISSUER in an exempt transaction or in specified exempt securities is excluded from the agent definition (no registration required).", "It is unlawful to transact business as an agent unless registered in the state or exempt (USA § 402).", "A broker-dealer may not employ an unregistered agent, and an agent may not work for an unregistered broker-dealer.", "An agent's registration is effective only while associated with a registered broker-dealer; it lapses when the association ends.", "When an agent changes firms, the agent, the former employer, and the new employer must all promptly notify the Administrator.", "Registration is filed on Form U4 through the CRD system; consent to service of process is included in the application.", "Form U4 requires disclosure of criminal, regulatory, civil-judicial, financial, and customer-complaint (disciplinary) history.", "Requirements to register generally include the application, fees, any required surety bond, and passing the appropriate qualification exam(s); net-capital requirements apply to the firm, not the agent.", "Post-registration: Form U4 must be amended promptly, generally within 30 days, when information becomes materially inaccurate.", "An agent registered in one state must separately register (or be exempt) to do business with clients in another state, even for existing customers who move."], "mnemonics": ["'Agents are people' - if it's an entity (BD/firm), it is NEVER an agent under § 401.", "U-4 = registration IN (getting into the business); U-5 = termination/OUT."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing the broker-dealer with an agent: the firm registers as a broker-dealer, only its individual representatives register as agents.", "Assuming ALL issuer representatives must register - those effecting exempt transactions or specified exempt securities are excluded from the agent definition.", "Thinking a receptionist or back-office clerk is an agent - they are not, unless they solicit or take securities orders.", "Believing registration follows the agent between firms automatically - it does not; the agent must re-register with the new BD, and all parties must notify the Administrator.", "Forgetting that an agent registered in State A cannot solicit a client physically located in State B merely because the client is an existing customer - state registration (or an exemption) is still required.", "Missing the ongoing U4 amendment duty: material changes (e.g., a new criminal charge, a customer complaint, a bankruptcy) must be reported promptly, generally within 30 days, not just at initial registration.", "Applying net-capital requirements to agents: net capital is a broker-dealer (firm) requirement; only a surety bond may be imposed on an individual agent (typically one with custody or discretion)."]},
  "law-securities": {"summary": "This component covers what counts as a \"security\" and \"issuer,\" how securities get registered (or exempted) at both the federal and state levels, what makes an entity an investment company, and the broad antifraud powers state regulators retain over any security. Under Uniform Securities Act (USA) Section 401, \"security\" is defined expansively to reach notes, stocks, bonds, investment contracts, and interests in oil/gas and other schemes, while an \"issuer\" is the person who issues or proposes to issue the security. The Howey investment-contract test (money invested in a common enterprise with an expectation of profits derived from the efforts of others) is the key tool for classifying novel instruments.\n\nRegistration operates on two tracks. Federally, Securities Act of 1933 Section 5 requires that non-exempt securities be registered with the SEC before public sale, enforced through the prospectus and quiet-period rules. Regulation D (Rules 504/506) provides safe-harbor private-placement exemptions, though only Rule 506 offerings are federal covered securities. The National Securities Markets Improvement Act (NSMIA) created \"federal covered securities\" (e.g., exchange-listed securities and investment company shares under SA Section 18), which states may NOT require to register; for investment company shares and Rule 506 offerings, states may still require a notice filing and fee, while for exchange-listed securities the state is essentially limited to its retained antifraud authority. At the state level, the USA offers three methods: registration by notification (§ 302), coordination (§ 303, filed simultaneously with a federal 1933 Act registration), and qualification (§ 304, the most demanding, for intrastate/non-federal offerings). Sections 301, 305, and 402 govern the registration requirement, procedures, and exempt securities/transactions.\n\nThe Investment Company Act of 1940 defines an \"investment company\" in Section 3 and classifies it (Section 4) into three types: face-amount certificate companies, unit investment trusts (UITs), and management companies, the last subclassified (Section 5) into open-end (mutual funds) and closed-end funds, and into diversified and non-diversified. The 75-5-10 rule defines a diversified fund. Finally, even when a security is federal covered or otherwise exempt from state registration, the state Administrator retains full antifraud authority under the USA (§ 501 and related) to investigate and sanction fraud in connection with the offer or sale of any security.", "keyPoints": ["USA § 401 defines \"security\" broadly (notes, stock, bonds, investment contracts, oil/gas interests); an \"issuer\" is the person who issues or proposes to issue a security.", "The Howey test finds an investment contract where there is (1) an investment of money, (2) in a common enterprise, (3) with an expectation of profit, (4) derived primarily from the efforts of others.", "Items NOT securities: insurance/fixed annuity contracts, commodities/precious metals themselves, collectibles, currency, and retirement plan interests like IRAs/Keoghs (the plan itself).", "SA § 5 bars offers/sales of non-exempt securities until an SEC registration statement is effective; a prospectus must be delivered.", "SA § 18 / NSMIA: \"federal covered securities\" (e.g., exchange-listed securities, investment company shares, and Rule 506 offerings) cannot be required to register by states; for investment company shares and Rule 506 offerings states may require a notice filing and fee, and states always retain antifraud authority.", "Reg D Rule 506(b)/(c) offerings are federal covered securities; states can only require a notice filing (often a copy of Form D) plus a fee, not registration. Rule 504 offerings are NOT federal covered, so states may require their registration.", "State registration methods (USA): notification (§ 302), coordination (§ 303, paired with a federal 1933 Act filing), and qualification (§ 304, most rigorous, for offerings not registered federally).", "Coordination generally becomes effective at the same moment the federal registration becomes effective; qualification becomes effective when the Administrator so orders.", "The ICA classifies three investment company types (§ 4): face-amount certificate companies, unit investment trusts (UITs), and management companies (open-end/closed-end), with \"investment company\" defined in § 3 and management companies subclassified in § 5.", "A \"diversified\" management company meets the 75-5-10 test; open-end funds (mutual funds) continuously issue redeemable shares, while closed-end funds issue a fixed number that trade in the secondary market.", "State antifraud authority survives federal preemption: the Administrator may pursue fraud in the offer or sale of ANY security, even a federal covered or exempt one.", "Exempt securities (e.g., U.S. government, municipal, bank issues) and exempt transactions are excused from state REGISTRATION, but never from the antifraud provisions."], "formulas": ["Howey investment contract = investment of money + common enterprise + expectation of profit + efforts of others (all four required).", "Diversified fund 75-5-10 rule: at least 75% of assets in the diversified bucket, no more than 5% of total assets in any one issuer, and no more than 10% of any one issuer's outstanding voting securities."], "mnemonics": ["Howey = \"I See Common Profit Others\" (Investment, Common enterprise, Profit expectation, Others' efforts).", "State registration methods NCQ = Notification, Coordination, Qualification (increasing order of scrutiny).", "Exemptions kill REGISTRATION, never FRAUD - antifraud always applies."], "pitfalls": ["Assuming a federal covered security is fully exempt from the state: states cannot require registration, but may require notice filings/fees for investment company and Rule 506 offerings, and always retain antifraud jurisdiction.", "Confusing coordination (state filing paired with a federal 1933 Act registration) with qualification (used when there is no federal registration, e.g., intrastate offerings).", "Thinking fixed annuities, commodities, precious metals, or an IRA itself are 'securities' - they are not; variable annuities and investment contracts are.", "Mixing up open-end (redeemable, continuously offered) with closed-end (fixed share count, trades at a premium/discount to NAV) management companies.", "Believing an exemption from registration also exempts the issuer from antifraud liability - it never does.", "Applying the 75-5-10 test to the whole portfolio - it governs only 75% of assets; the remaining 25% is unrestricted."]},
  "law-remedies": {"summary": "The remedies and administrative provisions of the Uniform Securities Act (USA) define how a state securities Administrator enforces the law and what penalties violators face. The Administrator's authority (§§ 204, 306, 406, 407, 412) includes conducting investigations inside or outside the state, subpoenaing witnesses and records, administering oaths, issuing stop orders on securities registrations, and denying, suspending, revoking, canceling, or barring the registrations of persons. Crucially, the Administrator is an administrative officer: it can issue cease and desist orders and summary orders on its own, but it CANNOT issue injunctions or send anyone to jail. Those remedies require the courts, so the Administrator must apply to a court for an injunction (§ 407) and refer criminal matters to the state Attorney General or a prosecutor.\n\nAdministrative actions (§ 408) let the Administrator issue orders that are in the public interest AND supported by a specific enumerated cause. Cease and desist orders may be issued with or without a prior hearing. Summary orders (e.g., suspension pending a final determination) can take effect immediately, but the affected person is entitled to a hearing, which the Administrator must hold within 15 days of a written request. Final orders require notice, opportunity for a hearing, and written findings of fact and conclusions of law. A person aggrieved by a final order may seek judicial review by filing in the appropriate court within 60 days; filing does not automatically stay the order.\n\nOther penalties and liabilities cover criminal, civil, and related consequences. Criminal penalties (§ 409) for a willful violation are a maximum $5,000 fine and/or 3 years imprisonment; no imprisonment is imposed if the defendant proves no knowledge of the rule or order violated, and the statute of limitations for criminal prosecution is 5 years. Civil liability (§ 410) gives a defrauded buyer a right of rescission: recovery equals the consideration paid plus interest, plus costs and reasonable attorney's fees, minus any income received on the security (upon tender of the security). The civil statute of limitations is the sooner of 3 years after the sale or 2 years after discovery of the violation. A seller can cut off liability by making a written rescission offer; if the buyer does not accept it within 30 days, the right to sue is lost.", "keyPoints": ["The Administrator can investigate within OR outside its state, subpoena witnesses/records, administer oaths, and compel sworn testimony (§ 406).", "The Administrator CANNOT issue injunctions or imprison anyone — it must apply to a court for an injunction (§ 407) and refer criminal cases to the AG/prosecutor.", "The Administrator may deny, suspend, revoke, cancel, censure, bar, or restrict registrations of persons (§ 204) and issue stop orders against securities registrations (§ 306); an order must be in the public interest AND rest on an enumerated cause.", "Cease and desist orders may be issued by the Administrator with or without a prior hearing — no court order is needed (§ 408).", "A summary order can take effect immediately, but the person is entitled to a hearing within 15 days of a written request (§ 412).", "Final orders require notice, opportunity for a hearing, and written findings of fact and conclusions of law.", "Judicial review of a final order must be sought by filing in court within 60 days; filing does not automatically stay the order.", "Criminal penalty for a willful violation: maximum $5,000 fine and/or 3 years in prison (§ 409).", "No imprisonment if the defendant proves he had no knowledge of the rule or order; the criminal statute of limitations is 5 years.", "Civil recovery (rescission) = consideration paid + interest + costs + reasonable attorney's fees − income received, upon tender of the security (§ 410).", "Civil statute of limitations: the SOONER of 3 years after the sale or 2 years after discovery of the violation.", "A written rescission offer that the buyer fails to accept within 30 days extinguishes the buyer's right to sue."], "formulas": ["Civil recovery, buyer still owns the security: Recovery = Consideration paid + Interest (at rate set by Administrator) + Court costs + Reasonable attorney's fees − Income received on the security (tendered back to seller).", "Civil recovery, buyer already sold the security: Damages = Consideration paid + Interest + Costs + Attorney's fees − Income received − Amount received on the disposition."], "mnemonics": ["Criminal '5 & 3, SOL 5': $5,000 fine, 3 years prison, 5-year statute of limitations.", "Civil 'SOL 3 or 2': sooner of 3 years after sale OR 2 years after discovery.", "Timelines: hearing on a summary order = 15 days; accept a rescission offer = 30 days; judicial review = 60 days.", "'The Administrator ADMINISTERS; the courts ENJOIN and IMPRISON.'"], "pitfalls": ["Thinking the Administrator can issue an injunction or jail a violator — both require a court; the Administrator only applies to the court or refers the case.", "Reversing the civil formula: you ADD interest and SUBTRACT income received on the security; students often do the opposite.", "Assuming a summary suspension is invalid without a prior hearing — summary orders are allowed; the hearing right kicks in on written request (within 15 days).", "Forgetting the 30-day rescission-offer rule: if the buyer does not accept a valid written offer within 30 days, the right to sue is lost.", "Believing 'willful' means intent to break the law — it only means the person intended the act; ignorance of the law is not a defense to the violation (though lack of knowledge can bar imprisonment).", "Mixing up statutes of limitations: civil is 3/2 years (sooner), criminal is 5 years."]},
  "law-comms": {"summary": "Investment advisers and their representatives are fiduciaries, and everything they say to clients and prospects is governed by that duty plus specific antifraud and advertising rules. The core obligation is full and fair disclosure of all material facts and conflicts of interest. Federal-covered advisers deliver Form ADV Part 2 (the brochure and brochure supplement) at or before entering the advisory contract and then annually within 120 days of fiscal year-end. Beyond disclosure, three categories of statements are policed heavily: representations about registration, guarantees of performance, and the terms baked into advisory contracts.\n\nUnder the Uniform Securities Act it is unlawful to state or imply that registration (of a person or a security) means the Administrator or SEC has approved, recommended, or passed upon the merits or qualifications involved. Truthfully stating that one is registered is fine; suggesting registration equals a government endorsement is fraud. Guaranteeing a specific return or guaranteeing against loss is a prohibited/unethical practice for advisers regardless of how confident the adviser is or whether the client consents.\n\nAdvisory contracts are shaped by IAA § 205, USA § 102 (antifraud), and the NASAA model rule: they should be in writing, describe services and the fee formula, prohibit assignment without client consent, require notice of any change in a partnership's membership, and generally bar performance-based (share-of-gains) fees except for 'qualified clients.' Finally, all correspondence and advertising — including websites, email, and social media — are advertisements or communications subject to the antifraud rules, SEC Rule 206(4)-1 (the amended Marketing Rule), and Rule 204-2 recordkeeping. The amended Marketing Rule now permits testimonials and endorsements if compensation and conflicts are clearly disclosed, reversing the old blanket ban.", "keyPoints": ["Investment advisers are fiduciaries; the baseline communication duty is full and fair disclosure of all material facts and conflicts of interest.", "Brochure rule: deliver Form ADV Part 2A (and 2B supplement) at or before entering the advisory contract, then annually within 120 days of fiscal year-end (delivering the updated brochure, or a summary of material changes plus an offer to provide it).", "It is unlawful to represent that registration of a person or security means the Administrator/SEC has approved, endorsed, or passed on the merits or qualifications (USA § 405).", "Stating truthfully that one is registered is permitted; implying registration = government approval is prohibited.", "Advisers may not guarantee a specific return or guarantee against loss; performance guarantees are a prohibited/unethical practice even with client consent.", "Advisory contracts should be written and disclose services, fees and the fee formula, the amount of any prepaid fee refundable on termination, and contract term.", "No assignment of an advisory contract without client consent; a partnership adviser must notify clients of any change in a majority of its membership (IAA § 205).", "Performance-based (share of capital gains/appreciation) fees are generally prohibited except for 'qualified clients' under SEC Rule 205-3 (roughly $1.1M AUM with the adviser or $2.2M net worth).", "Websites, email, and social media are advertisements/communications subject to antifraud rules (USA § 102), SEC Rule 206(4)-1, and the USA § 403 sales/advertising literature provisions.", "The amended SEC Marketing Rule (206(4)-1) permits testimonials and endorsements only with clear and prominent disclosure of compensation and material conflicts of interest.", "Rule 204-2 requires advertisements and communications to be retained, generally 5 years (first 2 in an easily accessible place).", "The SEC and state Administrators do not 'approve' or pre-clear advertisements; claiming otherwise is itself a violation."], "mnemonics": ["Registration = permission, NOT approval — you can say you're registered, never that the regulator blessed the merits.", "GUARANTEE = GONE: an adviser guaranteeing return or 'no loss' is an automatic violation.", "Qualified client for performance fees: think '1.1 in / 2.2 net' ($1.1M AUM or $2.2M net worth) — not the accredited-investor income test."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing the 120-day annual brochure delivery (measured from the adviser's fiscal year-end) with initial delivery, which must be at or before contract signing.", "Mixing up 'qualified client' (performance-fee eligibility: ~$1.1M AUM / $2.2M net worth) with 'accredited investor' ($200k/$300k income or $1M net worth excluding primary residence) — the income test does not make someone a qualified client.", "Assuming testimonials are still banned — the amended Marketing Rule permits them with proper disclosure; the trap is picking 'strictly prohibited.'", "Believing a client can waive the guarantee prohibition or consent to a performance guarantee — consent does not cure a prohibited practice; nonwaiver clauses void such waivers.", "Thinking regulators review or approve ads or registrations — they do neither; representing approval is a violation.", "Forgetting that social media, blogs, and firm websites count as advertising and must be retained and kept truthful just like print material."]},
  "law-ethics": {"summary": "An investment adviser (IA) is a fiduciary. It owes clients a duty of loyalty and a duty of care, which together require full and fair disclosure of all material facts, avoidance or disclosure of conflicts of interest, and acting in the client's best interest at all times. These duties flow from the antifraud provisions of the Investment Advisers Act of 1940 (Sections 206(1), 206(2), 206(3), 206(4)) and the parallel NASAA Unethical Business Practices model rules under the Uniform Securities Act. An adviser's fiduciary obligation cannot be waived by contract, so a 'hedge clause' purporting to waive it is itself a violation.\n\nCompensation rules are heavily tested. Performance-based fees are generally prohibited unless the client is a 'qualified client' (roughly $1.1M+ managed by the adviser or $2.2M+ net worth, excluding primary residence). Soft-dollar arrangements are permitted only within the safe harbor of Securities Exchange Act Section 28(e), which covers brokerage and research that provides lawful and appropriate assistance in investment decision-making, but NOT overhead items like office rent, computers, travel, or salaries. Pay-to-play (SEC Rule 206(4)-5) imposes a two-year 'time out' on receiving compensation for advising a government client after a covered associate makes a disqualifying political contribution above the de minimis ($350 to a candidate the person can vote for; $150 otherwise). MSRB Rule G-37 is the parallel pay-to-play rule for municipal securities professionals.\n\nCustody is defined broadly: it means holding client funds/securities or having any authority to obtain possession of them, including direct fee deduction, serving as trustee, or having a general power of attorney. Under SEC Rule 206(4)-2 and NASAA model rules, advisers with custody must use a qualified custodian, ensure clients get account statements at least quarterly, and (under the NASAA model) undergo an annual surprise examination by an independent accountant and maintain higher minimum net worth. Discretion (authority over what/how much/when to trade) is not the same as custody. Other core duties: suitability, the Uniform Prudent Investor Act (prudence judged at the portfolio level using modern portfolio theory, duty to diversify, ability to delegate), Regulation S-P privacy safeguards and initial/annual privacy notices, Rule 204A-1 code of ethics with access-person personal-trading reports, Section 206(3) principal/agency-cross trade disclosure and consent, and the NASAA business continuity and succession model rule requiring every adviser to maintain a written plan.", "keyPoints": ["An investment adviser is a fiduciary owing duties of loyalty and care; material conflicts must be eliminated or fully and fairly disclosed, and the fiduciary duty cannot be waived by a hedge clause.", "Performance-based fees are permitted only for 'qualified clients' (about $1.1M under management with the adviser or $2.2M net worth excluding primary residence), or for private-fund/qualified-purchaser situations.", "Soft dollars are lawful only inside the SEA Section 28(e) safe harbor and cover only brokerage and research; overhead such as rent, hardware, travel, salaries, and marketing falls outside it.", "SEC Rule 206(4)-5 pay-to-play imposes a two-year compensation 'time out' for advising a government entity after a triggering political contribution over the de minimis ($350 if the contributor can vote for the candidate, otherwise $150).", "MSRB Rule G-37 is the pay-to-play rule for municipal securities dealers/professionals, paralleling the adviser rule for advisory relationships with government issuers.", "Custody includes any authority to possess client assets: physical possession, serving as trustee, general POA, and (except for narrow fee-deduction relief) automatic deduction of advisory fees.", "Under the NASAA custody model rule an adviser with custody must use a qualified custodian, deliver at least quarterly statements, undergo an annual surprise audit, and meet a higher minimum net worth (or bond).", "Discretionary authority is the power to decide security, amount, or action without prior client approval each trade; time-and-price-only discretion does not require written discretionary authorization.", "The Uniform Prudent Investor Act judges prudence at the whole-portfolio level, incorporates modern portfolio theory, requires diversification unless special circumstances, and permits delegation of investment functions.", "SEC Rule 204A-1 requires advisers to adopt a code of ethics; 'access persons' must report personal securities holdings and transactions and pre-clear IPOs and limited (private) offerings.", "Regulation S-P requires safeguarding nonpublic personal information and delivering an initial and annual privacy notice with opt-out rights before sharing with unaffiliated third parties.", "Insider trading (trading on material nonpublic information), selling away, market manipulation (e.g., matched orders, painting the tape), front running, and churning are prohibited practices.", "NASAA's model rule requires every adviser to create and maintain a written business continuity and succession plan addressing protection of records, alternate means of communication, and orderly transition on the death/incapacity of key personnel.", "Agency cross and principal trades require disclosure and client consent before completion of the transaction under Advisers Act Section 206(3)."], "formulas": ["Qualified client thresholds (for performance fees): approximately $1,100,000 assets managed by the adviser OR $2,200,000 net worth (excluding primary residence).", "Pay-to-play de minimis: $350 per election to a candidate the contributor is entitled to vote for; $150 per election otherwise; exceeding these can trigger a 2-year compensation time out.", "Custody surprise exam / statements (NASAA model): qualified custodian + account statements at least quarterly + annual surprise examination by an independent accountant."], "mnemonics": ["CUSTODY red flags = 'PATT': Possession of assets, Automatic fee deduction, Trustee capacity, general POA / Third-party check authority.", "28(e) OK vs. NOT-OK: 'Research & Brokerage in, Rent & Rides out' (research and trade execution qualify; rent, computers, travel do not).", "Fiduciary core = 'LC': Loyalty (avoid/disclose conflicts) + Care (suitable, prudent, best execution)."], "pitfalls": ["Confusing discretion with custody: an adviser can have discretion without custody, and having custody triggers the surprise-audit and net-worth requirements, not discretion alone.", "Assuming any wealthy client qualifies for performance fees; the client must meet the qualified-client dollar thresholds (or be a qualified purchaser), not merely be 'accredited.'", "Thinking a hedge clause or client consent can waive the adviser's fiduciary duty; it cannot, and using one is itself a prohibited/unethical practice.", "Treating overhead (rent, computers, travel, salaries, general software) as soft-dollar eligible; only research and brokerage execution fall inside the Section 28(e) safe harbor.", "Believing disclosure alone always cures a principal or agency-cross trade; Section 206(3) also requires the client's consent obtained before the transaction is completed.", "Assuming direct deduction of advisory fees is never custody; it is custody, though the NASAA/SEC framework provides limited relief when specific safeguards (e.g., invoice to client/custodian) are met."]},
};
